1
00:00:03,412 --> 00:00:15,380
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm talking to Mr. Ray Hayworth in his den, and what I like to do in these interviews is usually start in right with the beginning, where you were born and what the family background was.

2
00:00:24,666 --> 00:00:32,992
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm talking to Mr. Ray Hayworth in his den, and what I like to do in these interviews is usually start in right with the beginning,

3
00:00:33,817 --> 00:00:39,578
[SPEAKER_01]: where you were born and what the family background was and how you grew up and first played ball and so on.

4
00:00:39,638 --> 00:00:42,519
[SPEAKER_01]: So if you'd start right in on that, that would be fine.

5
00:00:42,619 --> 00:00:52,540
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I can begin by saying that I grew up about 20 minutes from my home here, right near Springfield down here, which is a Quaker settlement.

6
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:56,181
[SPEAKER_00]: And all my line of people are Quakers.

7
00:00:56,761 --> 00:01:02,782
[SPEAKER_00]: And as I grew up around Springfield, even in

8
00:01:03,960 --> 00:01:04,760
[SPEAKER_00]: Great school.

9
00:01:06,681 --> 00:01:07,802
[SPEAKER_00]: Baseball was everything.

10
00:01:08,322 --> 00:01:10,784
[SPEAKER_00]: All the kids played baseball.

11
00:01:11,004 --> 00:01:20,108
[SPEAKER_00]: And we had leagues here at that time at every crossroads, every furniture factory mill had a baseball team.

12
00:01:20,148 --> 00:01:21,929
[SPEAKER_00]: So we kind of grew up in it.

13
00:01:21,969 --> 00:01:28,413
[SPEAKER_00]: And I might say this at the outset, in my early years, I grew up in kind of a baseball family.

14
00:01:28,973 --> 00:01:31,134
[SPEAKER_00]: There were four of us brothers.

15
00:01:32,018 --> 00:01:35,961
[SPEAKER_00]: And all of us played professionally, and all four of us were catchers.

16
00:01:36,762 --> 00:01:45,649
[SPEAKER_00]: And my brother, Red, followed me, which he's a younger member of our family, on into St. Louis, and he played, caught in the 44 World Series.

17
00:01:46,650 --> 00:02:01,362
[SPEAKER_00]: But getting back to my early days in, as I recall, around Springfield and south of High Point here, was really sort of the foundation of our baseball

18
00:02:01,793 --> 00:02:03,214
[SPEAKER_00]: beginning.

19
00:02:04,014 --> 00:02:06,016
[SPEAKER_00]: It was so interesting.

20
00:02:06,036 --> 00:02:14,821
[SPEAKER_00]: We had one old gentleman here they called Rubell, he was the Duke of Spiro, and he was a great left-hand pitcher in this area.

21
00:02:14,881 --> 00:02:22,526
[SPEAKER_00]: He sort of was our father along the way, and we all followed him and created so much interest in baseball.

22
00:02:22,546 --> 00:02:24,187
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you live on a farm, the family?

23
00:02:24,227 --> 00:02:26,989
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, we had a not too large a farm.

24
00:02:27,769 --> 00:02:30,631
[SPEAKER_00]: My dad was a tobacco farmer and raised cotton.

25
00:02:31,733 --> 00:02:36,896
[SPEAKER_00]: And he was a merchant for many years at a store.

26
00:02:36,936 --> 00:02:38,577
[SPEAKER_00]: Did he play any ball?

27
00:02:38,637 --> 00:02:39,778
[SPEAKER_00]: Never played in his life.

28
00:02:40,038 --> 00:02:43,500
[SPEAKER_00]: Didn't know a thing about baseball until we began playing.

29
00:02:44,500 --> 00:02:48,762
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, of course, he became intensely interested in baseball.

30
00:02:49,123 --> 00:02:49,903
[SPEAKER_00]: My mother, too.

31
00:02:50,043 --> 00:02:51,844
[SPEAKER_00]: Did they get to see you play in the big leagues?

32
00:02:51,884 --> 00:02:54,025
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, they used to come up to Washington.

33
00:02:54,566 --> 00:02:58,147
[SPEAKER_00]: My mother was up at the 34 World Series in Detroit, 35.

34
00:02:59,449 --> 00:03:00,749
[SPEAKER_00]: They really got to see some of us.

35
00:03:00,769 --> 00:03:00,929
[SPEAKER_00]: Great.

36
00:03:00,969 --> 00:03:02,410
[SPEAKER_01]: I just want to check and see how we're coming.

37
00:03:04,210 --> 00:03:09,332
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you hear about boys raised on the farm begin throwing rocks at tin cans?

38
00:03:10,352 --> 00:03:15,374
[SPEAKER_01]: I think that's where we developed our squirrel hut, throwing rocks at squirrels and one thing or another.

39
00:03:15,494 --> 00:03:23,216
[SPEAKER_00]: And I guess it does help to develop kids' arms according to the way you grew up.

40
00:03:24,197 --> 00:03:25,017
[SPEAKER_00]: But fortunately,

41
00:03:26,938 --> 00:03:31,522
[SPEAKER_00]: My brothers as well as myself, nature gave all four of us good strong arms.

42
00:03:31,542 --> 00:03:37,166
[SPEAKER_00]: We could all throw, which was very important, important in catching.

43
00:03:38,127 --> 00:03:42,010
[SPEAKER_00]: And we never had great foot speed.

44
00:03:42,570 --> 00:03:49,976
[SPEAKER_00]: The reason I'm saying these things, I learned so many of these things that you look for in all my years of scouting following my playing career.

45
00:03:51,237 --> 00:03:54,099
[SPEAKER_00]: And as time went on, I played in high school.

46
00:03:54,698 --> 00:03:58,339
[SPEAKER_00]: Eventually went to Oak Ridge Prep School close by here.

47
00:03:58,399 --> 00:03:59,999
[SPEAKER_00]: Excuse me, did they have a team in high school?

48
00:04:00,439 --> 00:04:00,699
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

49
00:04:01,219 --> 00:04:02,460
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, I played in high school.

50
00:04:02,500 --> 00:04:04,440
[SPEAKER_00]: Believe it or not, I pitched in high school.

51
00:04:05,160 --> 00:04:08,781
[SPEAKER_00]: And when I went to Oak Ridge, I began catching all together.

52
00:04:09,841 --> 00:04:12,242
[SPEAKER_00]: And my second year old, I signed with Detroit.

53
00:04:13,142 --> 00:04:18,243
[SPEAKER_01]: Was this Oak Ridge, was this an academy somewhat corresponding to high school years?

54
00:04:18,643 --> 00:04:23,144
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, it was a preparatory, it's more of a preparatory school for college.

55
00:04:23,675 --> 00:04:24,876
[SPEAKER_00]: You come off of high school.

56
00:04:25,036 --> 00:04:29,980
[SPEAKER_00]: I left high school in the 10th grade and went over there and completed my schooling there.

57
00:04:30,480 --> 00:04:35,564
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't go on to college because I chose to go into professional baseball at that point.

58
00:04:36,705 --> 00:04:40,849
[SPEAKER_00]: And I did sign with Detroit.

59
00:04:40,989 --> 00:04:45,012
[SPEAKER_00]: And at that time, they put a lot of their kids out at Bluefield, West Virginia.

60
00:04:45,612 --> 00:04:46,993
[SPEAKER_00]: And we played out there two years.

61
00:04:47,033 --> 00:04:51,457
[SPEAKER_00]: Rick Sarl and a group of us at Detroit had signed, which they

62
00:04:51,837 --> 00:04:54,277
[SPEAKER_00]: was due then, it was sort of head of the farm system.

63
00:04:55,618 --> 00:05:03,059
[SPEAKER_00]: And we played there, and then I came on with Detroit in 26, my first year in professional ball.

64
00:05:03,079 --> 00:05:06,420
[SPEAKER_01]: Tell me about all these players coming out of this area now.

65
00:05:06,460 --> 00:05:08,420
[SPEAKER_01]: The academy there, I guess?

66
00:05:09,180 --> 00:05:10,421
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, a lot of them.

67
00:05:11,501 --> 00:05:19,823
[SPEAKER_00]: Colonel Holt, the coach over there, which is highly regarded during his lifetime as one of the best coaches in the country, even though he was in prep school.

68
00:05:22,657 --> 00:05:29,419
[SPEAKER_00]: He sent many players to the major leagues from there, J.K. May and Wes Ferrell, my brother Red.

69
00:05:29,879 --> 00:05:37,600
[SPEAKER_00]: And of course, in fact, my brother Red was the ninth player that he sent to the major leagues from that little prep school.

70
00:05:38,921 --> 00:05:41,961
[SPEAKER_00]: And many other fellows went out of there and played.

71
00:05:42,082 --> 00:05:46,022
[SPEAKER_00]: So it was such a fun place.

72
00:05:46,062 --> 00:05:48,403
[SPEAKER_00]: And every kid in this country wanted to go to school at Oak Ridge.

73
00:05:49,693 --> 00:05:52,355
[SPEAKER_00]: They began seeing these fellows go out of there in the major leagues.

74
00:05:52,475 --> 00:05:56,959
[SPEAKER_01]: And so I just... How do you account for that?

75
00:05:56,979 --> 00:05:58,200
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a chance, I suppose.

76
00:05:58,360 --> 00:05:58,880
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

77
00:05:58,940 --> 00:06:00,541
[SPEAKER_00]: It's one generation producing.

78
00:06:00,642 --> 00:06:01,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

79
00:06:01,322 --> 00:06:08,968
[SPEAKER_00]: And at that time, when I moved into professional baseball, there were so many fellows right out of this immediate area.

80
00:06:09,108 --> 00:06:18,575
[SPEAKER_00]: Larry Woodall, Hoot Gibson, old Sam Gibson, the knuckleballer, and a great many fellows like the Ferrells.

81
00:06:19,080 --> 00:06:20,861
[SPEAKER_00]: where slaughter came on a little bit later.

82
00:06:20,962 --> 00:06:32,531
[SPEAKER_00]: And I know at one time we sat down and counted all the fellows from this area in here over about a 10-year period, and we could have gotten a major league club out of this immediate vicinity here.

83
00:06:33,852 --> 00:06:43,800
[SPEAKER_00]: And I think that was due to the fact, as I mentioned there briefly earlier, that so many fellows, kids, played ball in this area then.

84
00:06:43,920 --> 00:06:48,404
[SPEAKER_00]: And wherever there's a great deal of baseball play, prospects come out of that.

85
00:06:49,225 --> 00:07:05,015
[SPEAKER_00]: We learned that in later years in scouting that after we had covered the country here, we began going to Cuba and Puerto Rico and Panama and Dominican Republic because the kids down there, they played ball.

86
00:07:05,035 --> 00:07:09,398
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all they ever do is play ball and get a stick and make a ball and get out and play.

87
00:07:09,418 --> 00:07:12,560
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have other sports which take the attention of the boys here?

88
00:07:12,600 --> 00:07:12,800
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

89
00:07:14,183 --> 00:07:21,846
[SPEAKER_00]: They realize down there it's going on today that there's a great opportunity in baseball and they just go all out for it and that's all they do is play.

90
00:07:23,027 --> 00:07:26,268
[SPEAKER_01]: So... What year was it you finished up at the academy?

91
00:07:26,488 --> 00:07:27,509
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you remember?

92
00:07:27,909 --> 00:07:28,609
[SPEAKER_00]: Was that the...

93
00:07:28,869 --> 00:07:30,290
[SPEAKER_00]: In 25.

94
00:07:30,310 --> 00:07:33,512
[SPEAKER_00]: In 26 I went spring training with Detroit State.

95
00:07:33,572 --> 00:07:34,992
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, sorry.

96
00:07:35,172 --> 00:07:36,053
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, Benidorm.

97
00:07:36,813 --> 00:07:40,415
[SPEAKER_00]: Benidorm made one trip through this country one spring.

98
00:07:40,475 --> 00:07:43,696
[SPEAKER_00]: He stopped off at Wake Forest, signed Vic Sorrell,

99
00:07:44,505 --> 00:07:48,727
[SPEAKER_00]: He came through here, and I signed with Detroit at that time.

100
00:07:48,767 --> 00:07:52,388
[SPEAKER_00]: He went on out to Tennessee and got Dale Alexander and Johnny Stone.

101
00:07:52,909 --> 00:07:58,151
[SPEAKER_00]: He got us an array of players, and every one of us wound up in Detroit playing up there.

102
00:07:58,251 --> 00:08:00,792
[SPEAKER_00]: He saw you play here with the academy team?

103
00:08:00,812 --> 00:08:02,453
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, yes, at Oak Ridge.

104
00:08:03,293 --> 00:08:06,295
[SPEAKER_01]: Did he come and seek you out or go to your family?

105
00:08:07,095 --> 00:08:12,297
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, he visited with us, mainly through the coach at Oak Ridge.

106
00:08:14,565 --> 00:08:30,051
[SPEAKER_00]: I had an opportunity to go to Wake Forest to school, but at that time all I could see was baseball and I just sort of lived and admired fellas I'd read about all my life like Ty Cobb and Trish Speaker and Eddie Collins and Babe Ruth and all of them.

107
00:08:30,232 --> 00:08:33,493
[SPEAKER_00]: And I got to go up there and play against these fellas.

108
00:08:34,033 --> 00:08:37,935
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember the day, the first day I was in a ballgame, got to hit against Walter Johnson.

109
00:08:37,995 --> 00:08:39,615
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought that was the greatest thing there was.

110
00:08:41,303 --> 00:08:45,246
[SPEAKER_01]: But did your parents want you to go on to college, or how did they feel about it?

111
00:08:45,286 --> 00:08:52,572
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, they didn't push college too much because I really didn't turn in that direction.

112
00:08:52,972 --> 00:08:56,255
[SPEAKER_00]: I just wasn't interested at that point in going to college.

113
00:08:57,296 --> 00:09:01,880
[SPEAKER_00]: And fortunately, I went right on into professional ball.

114
00:09:01,940 --> 00:09:02,920
[SPEAKER_01]: How'd they feel about that?

115
00:09:02,940 --> 00:09:03,761
[SPEAKER_01]: Did they like that?

116
00:09:03,781 --> 00:09:04,882
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, yes.

117
00:09:04,902 --> 00:09:05,963
[SPEAKER_01]: Did they have to sign?

118
00:09:05,983 --> 00:09:07,264
[SPEAKER_01]: Were you engaged to sign?

119
00:09:07,284 --> 00:09:08,005
[SPEAKER_01]: They signed for me.

120
00:09:11,492 --> 00:09:15,853
[SPEAKER_01]: Was the idea to go right up and you went to the Tigers right away?

121
00:09:15,993 --> 00:09:18,054
[SPEAKER_01]: In 26, and I stayed up there nearly all year.

122
00:09:18,254 --> 00:09:19,234
[SPEAKER_01]: Tell me about that now.

123
00:09:19,254 --> 00:09:21,135
[SPEAKER_00]: What did it feel like going up to the big city there?

124
00:09:21,315 --> 00:09:22,935
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I'll tell you how this happened.

125
00:09:23,255 --> 00:09:24,796
[SPEAKER_00]: I went to spring training.

126
00:09:24,956 --> 00:09:29,877
[SPEAKER_00]: Toronto, their AAA club in Detroit, both trained at Augusta, Georgia.

127
00:09:31,077 --> 00:09:35,199
[SPEAKER_00]: And I went down with the Toronto club, Vic Sorrell and I, and we went through spring training.

128
00:09:36,199 --> 00:09:40,360
[SPEAKER_00]: And I caught a few games in Toronto.

129
00:09:41,653 --> 00:09:50,417
[SPEAKER_00]: And unfortunately at that point for the Detroit club, but fortunately for me, Johnny Baxter broke his leg.

130
00:09:51,217 --> 00:09:51,657
[SPEAKER_00]: Baxter?

131
00:09:52,118 --> 00:09:54,839
[SPEAKER_00]: Johnny Baxter was their number one catcher in Detroit.

132
00:09:55,759 --> 00:09:59,741
[SPEAKER_00]: And after he broke his leg, they were looking around.

133
00:09:59,961 --> 00:10:05,223
[SPEAKER_00]: So they had seen me in spring training and Ty Cobb said, well let's just bring that kid over here.

134
00:10:06,143 --> 00:10:08,924
[SPEAKER_00]: and let him stay here until Bassler gets back.

135
00:10:08,984 --> 00:10:11,464
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, Bassler didn't come back until August.

136
00:10:12,384 --> 00:10:18,685
[SPEAKER_00]: In the meantime, after I got up there, Larry Woodall was the other catcher, and he got a terrible spike wound in his knee.

137
00:10:19,665 --> 00:10:21,886
[SPEAKER_00]: So they looked around, and I'm all that's left sitting there.

138
00:10:21,926 --> 00:10:25,686
[SPEAKER_00]: So I began catching some game, and I got along real well.

139
00:10:26,687 --> 00:10:34,908
[SPEAKER_00]: And it was a great experience for me, because I knew after playing up there that I had no need to go out and play.

140
