148
00:11:09,832 --> 00:11:15,696
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, Casey will teach you to hit the right field, hit and run, and things you're going to need to do to compete up here.

149
00:11:16,637 --> 00:11:19,559
[SPEAKER_00]: So I went on down there with Casey.

150
00:11:20,160 --> 00:11:21,421
[SPEAKER_00]: I wound up with a great year.

151
00:11:21,441 --> 00:11:28,786
[SPEAKER_00]: I hit 330, went back to Detroit, and was there almost 10 years before I was sold to Brooklyn.

152
00:11:29,507 --> 00:11:31,529
[SPEAKER_01]: What was it like going up there in 26?

153
00:11:31,569 --> 00:11:33,250
[SPEAKER_01]: And here's Ty Cobb, your manager now.

154
00:11:34,090 --> 00:11:35,191
[SPEAKER_01]: What did you think?

155
00:11:35,211 --> 00:11:36,771
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it was a great thrill for me.

156
00:11:36,831 --> 00:11:42,714
[SPEAKER_00]: And two, at that point, I had read so much about the stars up there.

157
00:11:43,674 --> 00:11:52,638
[SPEAKER_00]: I mentioned a while ago Eddie Collins and Trist Speaker and Walter Johnson and all the old timers that were around the league then.

158
00:11:53,618 --> 00:11:55,959
[SPEAKER_00]: And seeing them was a great thrill to me.

159
00:11:56,099 --> 00:11:59,161
[SPEAKER_00]: And then getting to actually play against them.

160
00:12:00,341 --> 00:12:02,222
[SPEAKER_00]: And so I did a

161
00:12:02,645 --> 00:12:05,827
[SPEAKER_00]: A lot for me, it made me want to get ready to play up there more than ever.

162
00:12:07,088 --> 00:12:10,310
[SPEAKER_00]: How did Cobb deal with you?

163
00:12:10,831 --> 00:12:14,273
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, Ty Cobb, I've been asked that question many times.

164
00:12:14,953 --> 00:12:19,416
[SPEAKER_00]: He was the most fierce competitor on the field that I ever saw.

165
00:12:19,597 --> 00:12:22,218
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't do anything to beat you.

166
00:12:23,139 --> 00:12:29,623
[SPEAKER_00]: But the nicest, mildest fellow to sit in the hotel lobby and visit with that you would ever want to meet.

167
00:12:29,783 --> 00:12:31,985
[SPEAKER_00]: You could never put the two of them together

168
00:12:32,803 --> 00:12:36,284
[SPEAKER_00]: And I used to listen to him talk, which they did a great deal in those days.

169
00:12:36,304 --> 00:12:38,084
[SPEAKER_00]: They'd sit around the lobby and talk.

170
00:12:39,125 --> 00:12:47,086
[SPEAKER_00]: I used to listen to the great stories Cobb would tell about base stealing and how they sharpened their spikes, which went on back in those days.

171
00:12:47,327 --> 00:12:49,107
[SPEAKER_00]: The other team would walk by the dugout.

172
00:12:49,407 --> 00:12:51,267
[SPEAKER_00]: That's right.

173
00:12:52,208 --> 00:12:54,768
[SPEAKER_00]: But I was pleasantly surprised.

174
00:12:54,788 --> 00:12:56,069
[SPEAKER_00]: He was very nice to me.

175
00:12:56,089 --> 00:12:57,689
[SPEAKER_00]: He knew I was just a young kid.

176
00:12:58,836 --> 00:13:03,460
[SPEAKER_00]: And we developed a friendship that lasted all through the years as long as he lived.

177
00:13:03,700 --> 00:13:04,040
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

178
00:13:05,301 --> 00:13:09,024
[SPEAKER_00]: And other people in Detroit at that time were nice to me.

179
00:13:09,324 --> 00:13:12,627
[SPEAKER_00]: Charlie Garinger was just coming on, great career ahead of him.

180
00:13:13,428 --> 00:13:22,795
[SPEAKER_00]: And our Detroit teams, our great teams in there, 34 and 35 and on, began to be put together in a 32.

181
00:13:25,191 --> 00:13:33,033
[SPEAKER_00]: Players coming up from the Texas League, 33 and 34, Jojo White and Schoolboy Rowe and Tommy Bridges.

182
00:13:33,873 --> 00:13:35,574
[SPEAKER_00]: All those fellows were just coming on.

183
00:13:35,594 --> 00:13:37,174
[SPEAKER_00]: We all kind of grew up together there.

184
00:13:38,334 --> 00:13:53,498
[SPEAKER_00]: And when they were able to make the addition of Mickey Cochran and Goose Gosling, adding them to Hank Greenberg and Charlie Garinger and Marv Owens and Pete Fox and the great pitchers we had in there at that time.

185
00:13:53,925 --> 00:14:00,670
[SPEAKER_00]: Ellen Hawker and Hogsett and Sorrell and Crowder and Rowe and Bridges.

186
00:14:01,611 --> 00:14:05,234
[SPEAKER_00]: And I'll tell you, it was such a pleasure to play.

187
00:14:05,274 --> 00:14:06,695
[SPEAKER_00]: We just went every day nearly.

188
00:14:06,735 --> 00:14:09,617
[SPEAKER_00]: We went two out of three days.

189
00:14:10,318 --> 00:14:15,622
[SPEAKER_00]: But I learned how nice it was to play on a championship club.

190
00:14:15,642 --> 00:14:20,005
[SPEAKER_00]: And then I learned later how disappointing it was to play on a second division club.

191
00:14:21,233 --> 00:14:25,176
[SPEAKER_00]: So I had a taste of it all the way around.

192
00:14:25,196 --> 00:14:30,819
[SPEAKER_01]: Tell me, do you remember the scandal at the end of the 26th season where Cobb and Speaker had to quit and leave?

193
00:14:30,839 --> 00:14:32,500
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you remember much about that?

194
00:14:32,780 --> 00:14:34,421
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't remember too much.

195
00:14:34,501 --> 00:14:37,223
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember, yes, but not the details.

196
00:14:37,243 --> 00:14:43,207
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, Cobb had to, he resigned as manager rather early, I guess.

197
00:14:43,387 --> 00:14:48,390
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, he resigned that year, and of course he went on to Philadelphia with Connie Mack and played a little more,

198
00:14:49,181 --> 00:14:51,283
[SPEAKER_00]: He was near the end of his career then.

199
00:14:52,384 --> 00:15:00,750
[SPEAKER_00]: Believe it or not, the year I was born in 1904, Ty Cobb was playing in Detroit, and I finally grew up and got up there to play with him before he finished there.

200
00:15:01,471 --> 00:15:05,014
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it is...

201
00:15:05,034 --> 00:15:07,936
[SPEAKER_02]: Were you with them in 27?

202
00:15:07,956 --> 00:15:09,538
[SPEAKER_02]: You weren't with them though, so...

203
00:15:09,578 --> 00:15:12,440
[SPEAKER_01]: I was trying to get at here how the club felt.

204
00:15:13,101 --> 00:15:14,061
[SPEAKER_01]: after he was gone.

205
00:15:14,421 --> 00:15:15,822
[SPEAKER_01]: Did Donnie Bush manage in 27?

206
00:15:15,982 --> 00:15:17,782
[SPEAKER_01]: I think he did.

207
00:15:17,842 --> 00:15:19,803
[SPEAKER_01]: I wonder what the change in managers would be.

208
00:15:19,863 --> 00:15:21,663
[SPEAKER_00]: Moriarty came in there and managed.

209
00:15:22,003 --> 00:15:25,544
[SPEAKER_00]: And then following that, Bucky Harris came on.

210
00:15:26,144 --> 00:15:29,165
[SPEAKER_00]: I played for Cobb there.

211
00:15:29,225 --> 00:15:37,007
[SPEAKER_00]: Bucky Harris and Mickey Cochran, Dale Baker, all were the managers when I was there.

212
00:15:38,107 --> 00:15:40,268
[SPEAKER_00]: I really played for some great managers, I believe.

213
00:15:40,348 --> 00:15:41,688
[SPEAKER_00]: I went on to Brooklyn and played

214
00:15:42,512 --> 00:15:44,993
[SPEAKER_00]: with DeRosier, Bill Terry in New York.

215
00:15:45,113 --> 00:15:47,675
[SPEAKER_00]: Who's at the top of your list?

216
00:15:47,875 --> 00:15:49,476
[SPEAKER_00]: Southworth in St. Louis.

217
00:15:50,837 --> 00:15:54,719
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I thought Southworth was a great manager.

218
00:15:55,259 --> 00:15:57,640
[SPEAKER_00]: I wasn't with him too long.

219
00:15:57,700 --> 00:16:01,543
[SPEAKER_00]: Bucky Harris was a good manager, a good, solid manager.

220
00:16:01,583 --> 00:16:04,364
[SPEAKER_00]: And DeRosier was a good manager.

221
00:16:04,764 --> 00:16:11,408
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember Mr. Rickey saying in a meeting one time that DeRosier could make decisions faster

222
00:16:12,071 --> 00:16:17,593
[SPEAKER_00]: than any manager he ever knew, you know, how rapidly the situation changes during the course of a game.

223
00:16:18,113 --> 00:16:19,914
[SPEAKER_00]: And DeRosier was right there every second.

224
00:16:20,214 --> 00:16:22,735
[SPEAKER_00]: He admired him for that, so he really liked DeRosier so much.

225
00:16:23,676 --> 00:16:26,177
[SPEAKER_00]: But, uh... Their lifestyles weren't exactly the same.

226
00:16:26,257 --> 00:16:26,577
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

227
00:16:27,177 --> 00:16:29,478
[SPEAKER_01]: Much different at night and day, but...

228
00:16:29,498 --> 00:16:31,059
[SPEAKER_01]: He recognized his ability.

229
00:16:31,179 --> 00:16:33,740
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, DeRosier was a good manager.

230
00:16:33,760 --> 00:16:37,882
[SPEAKER_01]: Did Ty, did you get much chance to see Ty Cobb in the managerial role?

231
00:16:38,362 --> 00:16:38,642
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

232
00:16:40,227 --> 00:16:47,313
[SPEAKER_00]: I think Ty was such a great star, and he expected so much from his men because he was so great himself.

233
00:16:48,954 --> 00:16:55,800
[SPEAKER_00]: Other people like Southworth and Mickey Cochran and all, they had different turns to them in managing.

234
00:16:57,021 --> 00:16:58,442
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's just hard to say.

235
00:16:59,983 --> 00:17:07,009
[SPEAKER_00]: They were all good managers, but I just feel like that one or two of them were so outstanding in my mind.

236
00:17:08,785 --> 00:17:17,411
[SPEAKER_01]: It seems to be often the way a great player turned manager doesn't do that well, and the great managers are those who were never great players.

237
00:17:17,431 --> 00:17:19,773
[SPEAKER_01]: I'd like to quote Mr. Rickey again.

238
00:17:19,873 --> 00:17:31,701
[SPEAKER_00]: I had a great admiration for Mr. Rickey because I played for him, broke in scouting under him, and he analyzed the game so thoroughly.

239
00:17:32,582 --> 00:17:35,344
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said one time about managers,

240
00:17:36,061 --> 00:17:45,186
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, there is no way in the world to tell whether a fellow can manage or not, can become a manager, until you give him a club and put him on the field.

241
00:17:45,626 --> 00:17:46,807
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you can find out.

242
00:17:47,688 --> 00:17:51,129
[SPEAKER_00]: Meaning that you couldn't take a star and say, well, he'll be a great manager.

243
00:17:52,070 --> 00:17:54,952
[SPEAKER_00]: A lot of our great managers, some of them never played major league.

244
00:17:56,372 --> 00:17:57,133
[SPEAKER_00]: Walter Alston.

245
00:17:57,173 --> 00:18:02,536
[SPEAKER_00]: Walter Alston, and McCarthy, and New York, and a lot of those fellows.

246
00:18:06,412 --> 00:18:15,699
[SPEAKER_00]: Managers are a rare breed of people, and you just can't tell, as Mr. Rickey used to say, until you give them the opportunity to play.

247
00:18:18,161 --> 00:18:20,002
[SPEAKER_01]: What kind of a chap was Dale Alexander?

248
00:18:20,663 --> 00:18:22,004
[SPEAKER_00]: Great man.

249
00:18:22,044 --> 00:18:22,344
[SPEAKER_00]: Great.

250
00:18:22,664 --> 00:18:30,730
[SPEAKER_00]: I just thought, I roomed with Dale for several years, and he was a mild-mannered, easy-going fellow.

251
00:18:30,890 --> 00:18:32,672
[SPEAKER_00]: Boy, he could swing that bat.

252
00:18:34,412 --> 00:18:40,414
[SPEAKER_00]: He left our club in Detroit, went to Boston, led the American League, and hit him when he was as slow as a snozzin' Marty.

253
00:18:41,295 --> 00:18:42,835
[SPEAKER_00]: And he...

254
00:18:43,116 --> 00:18:45,437
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess the... was it Harry Davis came up?

255
00:18:45,777 --> 00:18:46,077
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

256
00:18:46,517 --> 00:18:48,258
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know if they'd kept him though.

257
00:18:48,318 --> 00:18:51,839
[SPEAKER_00]: But Dale was a real gentleman.

258
00:18:51,919 --> 00:18:55,181
[SPEAKER_00]: He was the finest character you'd ever want to meet.

259
00:18:57,482 --> 00:18:58,922
[SPEAKER_00]: He used to come over here to see me in.

260
00:18:59,182 --> 00:19:00,983
[SPEAKER_00]: I went home with him one year after the season.

261
00:19:01,003 --> 00:19:03,444
[SPEAKER_00]: Spent a week with that squirrel out in Midland and stuff.

262
00:19:06,878 --> 00:19:12,362
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, he and Johnny Stone and fellas like that, I was with in Detroit, Tommy Bridges, all of them.

263
00:19:12,422 --> 00:19:14,163
[SPEAKER_00]: There were Tennesseans up there, weren't there?

264
00:19:14,203 --> 00:19:14,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

265
00:19:15,344 --> 00:19:17,486
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, a lot of ballplayers come out of Tennessee.

266
00:19:19,668 --> 00:19:20,969
[SPEAKER_01]: You mentioned Johnny Gill.

267
00:19:22,190 --> 00:19:24,231
[SPEAKER_01]: Were you with him at some time?

268
00:19:24,392 --> 00:19:30,036
[SPEAKER_00]: I was with him in Shreveport, Louisiana, the Texas League, and he later went up to Cleveland.

269
00:19:31,567 --> 00:19:34,408
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember the day he left the club down there to join Cleveland.

270
00:19:34,448 --> 00:19:37,868
[SPEAKER_00]: He had his big suitcase with two bats strapped on the outside of it.

271
00:19:38,508 --> 00:19:43,869
[SPEAKER_00]: But he was a, everybody in baseball thought a great deal of him.

272
00:19:43,929 --> 00:19:45,810
[SPEAKER_00]: He had such a great sense of humor.

273
00:19:45,990 --> 00:19:49,770
[SPEAKER_00]: He just was the most likable kind of a fellow.

274
00:19:51,731 --> 00:19:58,912
[SPEAKER_00]: But along the way you met so many individuals that were so different.

275
00:19:59,954 --> 00:20:02,195
[SPEAKER_00]: naturally all over the country.

276
00:20:03,356 --> 00:20:13,361
[SPEAKER_00]: But some of the best friends I had came on later, like Lavegetto and people like that in Brooklyn that I got to know real well and associated with.

277
00:20:16,783 --> 00:20:21,126
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, tell me a little bit about Nashville.

278
00:20:22,346 --> 00:20:26,969
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I went to Nashville with Jimmy Hamilton.

279
00:20:28,209 --> 00:20:29,050
[SPEAKER_00]: And I played there.

280
00:20:29,190 --> 00:20:32,852
[SPEAKER_00]: And he had an outstanding catcher at that time named Leo Mackey.

281
00:20:33,652 --> 00:20:36,254
[SPEAKER_00]: And I just wasn't getting any chance to work at all.

282
00:20:36,494 --> 00:20:37,795
[SPEAKER_00]: And he was trying to be independent.

283
00:20:37,815 --> 00:20:40,237
[SPEAKER_00]: Naturally, they were independent from where I was going along.

284
00:20:40,277 --> 00:20:44,879
[SPEAKER_00]: So he had a friend over in Texas League at Shreveport named Art Phelan.

285
00:20:45,880 --> 00:20:48,402
[SPEAKER_00]: And Art needed a catcher.

286
00:20:49,282 --> 00:20:50,183
[SPEAKER_00]: And I wanted to play.

287
00:20:50,963 --> 00:20:52,344
[SPEAKER_00]: So they just sent me over there.

288
00:20:54,066 --> 00:20:56,387
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll just tell you quickly a most unusual story.

289
00:20:56,407 --> 00:20:57,408
[SPEAKER_00]: Tell us slowly.

290
00:20:57,848 --> 00:21:04,471
[SPEAKER_00]: I went over there and it was as hot as blazes down there.

291
00:21:04,491 --> 00:21:06,372
[SPEAKER_00]: It was all day ball.

292
00:21:07,413 --> 00:21:09,114
[SPEAKER_00]: And I didn't start off too good there.

293
00:21:09,474 --> 00:21:12,135
[SPEAKER_00]: It was so hot until I got in shape.

294
00:21:13,356 --> 00:21:17,558
[SPEAKER_00]: And I played down there two or three weeks and finally they called up and said,

295
00:21:20,293 --> 00:21:26,415
[SPEAKER_00]: told Detroit, called Jack Zeller, and he said, what about this Hayworth boy?

296
00:21:26,455 --> 00:21:28,256
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, well, he's not doing too well.

297
00:21:28,296 --> 00:21:30,877
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, well, just give him his release.

298
00:21:32,458 --> 00:21:36,459
[SPEAKER_00]: So we went on a road trip right at that time, down to San Antonio.

299
00:21:36,519 --> 00:21:39,460
[SPEAKER_00]: And I had the hottest streak I ever had in my life baseball.

300
00:21:39,580 --> 00:21:42,502
[SPEAKER_00]: I hit a bunch of home runs and hit something with the bases loaded.

301
00:21:44,162 --> 00:21:46,063
[SPEAKER_00]: After they had told him to release me,

302
00:21:46,972 --> 00:21:51,193
[SPEAKER_00]: The giant scout was following our club, and he saw me play and have that hot streak.

303
00:21:52,033 --> 00:21:55,674
[SPEAKER_00]: New York immediately called up Sreeport and offered $50,000 for me.

304
00:21:55,974 --> 00:21:58,754
[SPEAKER_00]: They called up Detroit and said, Evans, don't release him.

305
00:21:59,395 --> 00:22:01,375
[SPEAKER_00]: So that year I finished up.

306
00:22:01,455 --> 00:22:02,575
[SPEAKER_00]: Next year I had a good year.

307
00:22:02,595 --> 00:22:05,076
[SPEAKER_00]: The Zellerhead actually told you?

308
00:22:05,096 --> 00:22:05,876
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

309
00:22:05,936 --> 00:22:09,096
[SPEAKER_00]: So the next year around I had a good solid year.

310
00:22:09,336 --> 00:22:10,797
[SPEAKER_00]: What happened to you on that road trip?

311
00:22:11,377 --> 00:22:12,277
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I don't know.

312
00:22:12,297 --> 00:22:13,437
[SPEAKER_01]: The heat and everything else?

313
00:22:13,457 --> 00:22:15,178
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I caught up with that heat.

314
00:22:15,318 --> 00:22:16,158
[SPEAKER_00]: I got in shape.

315
00:22:17,754 --> 00:22:20,336
[SPEAKER_00]: I think it's in the Texas League Record Book, I believe.

316
00:22:20,436 --> 00:22:31,243
[SPEAKER_00]: I hit, I think it was three home runs with the bases loaded, and I hit a double and a triple, and just knocked in a whole slew of runs.

317
00:22:31,303 --> 00:22:33,864
[SPEAKER_00]: I think it was 21 runs on two base hits.

318
00:22:34,465 --> 00:22:36,866
[SPEAKER_00]: And this scout, he just couldn't pass that up.

319
00:22:36,966 --> 00:22:39,728
[SPEAKER_00]: So that really saved me there.

320
00:22:39,788 --> 00:22:41,949
[SPEAKER_00]: It gave me the opportunity to go ahead and play.

321
00:22:43,010 --> 00:22:45,452
[SPEAKER_00]: And as time went on, I got more experience.

322
00:22:45,792 --> 00:22:47,393
[SPEAKER_00]: I began to get a hold of things.

323
00:22:48,347 --> 00:22:54,530
[SPEAKER_00]: But the key to my going back to the Major Leagues was the year I spent with Casey Stingman, 29, actually.

324
00:22:54,570 --> 00:22:57,451
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, Casey can really teach you things.

325
00:22:57,531 --> 00:22:58,432
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, he was a great teacher.

326
00:22:58,472 --> 00:23:00,172
[SPEAKER_01]: Even though he didn't know how to talk too clearly.

327
00:23:00,733 --> 00:23:07,115
[SPEAKER_00]: I've always said that a fellow that had a career in baseball should have played at least one year for Casey Stingman.

328
00:23:07,596 --> 00:23:09,937
[SPEAKER_00]: He was the most remarkable character.

329
00:23:09,957 --> 00:23:11,978
[SPEAKER_00]: He used to have these meetings in the clubhouse.

330
00:23:13,581 --> 00:23:15,122
[SPEAKER_00]: Honestly, I couldn't keep a straight face.

331
00:23:15,202 --> 00:23:16,884
[SPEAKER_00]: I'd hide back in my locker.

332
00:23:16,924 --> 00:23:18,846
[SPEAKER_00]: He was the funniest man I ever listened to.

333
00:23:19,646 --> 00:23:21,008
[SPEAKER_00]: How did he get his points across?

334
00:23:21,028 --> 00:23:22,149
[SPEAKER_01]: But he'd get them across.

335
00:23:22,469 --> 00:23:23,410
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, he did.

336
00:23:23,850 --> 00:23:24,350
[SPEAKER_01]: Double talk.

337
00:23:24,370 --> 00:23:25,391
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess you knew what he meant.

338
00:23:25,431 --> 00:23:27,093
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you think he's put some of that on?

339
00:23:27,113 --> 00:23:27,653
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah.

340
00:23:27,673 --> 00:23:31,316
[SPEAKER_00]: He came to the clubhouse one day and got right in the middle of the clubhouse and called a meeting.

341
00:23:31,817 --> 00:23:34,179
[SPEAKER_00]: Sat there with a newspaper reading the stock market.

342
00:23:34,219 --> 00:23:35,460
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, I'll tell you, fellas.

343
00:23:36,321 --> 00:23:39,183
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, this railroad stock is beginning to look pretty good.

344
00:23:39,824 --> 00:23:41,085
[SPEAKER_00]: But he said it's going to go up.

345
00:23:41,517 --> 00:23:46,260
[SPEAKER_00]: because the way you fellas are playing, you're really going to be riding the rails pretty soon.

346
00:23:46,900 --> 00:23:49,342
[SPEAKER_00]: He made his point.

347
00:23:50,263 --> 00:23:55,566
[SPEAKER_00]: He used to get up in the middle of the clubhouse, right near a trunk and take a fungo bat.

348
00:23:56,586 --> 00:23:57,867
[SPEAKER_00]: And you know how he could lecture.

349
00:23:57,887 --> 00:23:58,888
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, he could talk.

350
00:23:59,228 --> 00:24:01,570
[SPEAKER_00]: Every once in a while he'd take a fungo bat and hit the trunk.

351
00:24:01,610 --> 00:24:03,511
[SPEAKER_00]: It sounded like a stick of dynamite going off.

352
00:24:06,231 --> 00:24:15,053
[SPEAKER_00]: I just want to say that any man that played baseball, if he could have one year with Casey Stinger, he was a great manager.

353
00:24:15,593 --> 00:24:19,134
[SPEAKER_01]: But he did it in such a way that everybody liked him.

354
00:24:19,374 --> 00:24:23,175
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, when he was with the Boston Bees, they were a second division ball club.

355
00:24:23,795 --> 00:24:24,135
[SPEAKER_01]: Well.

356
00:24:24,275 --> 00:24:26,256
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you do that much when the material is there?

357
00:24:26,376 --> 00:24:26,956
[SPEAKER_00]: That's right.

358
00:24:26,996 --> 00:24:27,936
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't do it.

359
00:24:28,476 --> 00:24:31,797
[SPEAKER_00]: We've always said in baseball, you've got to have the horses to win.

360
00:24:32,097 --> 00:24:34,338
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't win unless you do have it.

361
00:24:35,178 --> 00:24:36,018
[SPEAKER_00]: And I can see that.

362
00:24:36,662 --> 00:24:39,324
[SPEAKER_00]: mediocre clubs in the early 30s in Detroit.

363
00:24:39,364 --> 00:24:43,487
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the fellas grew up and we got adding stars.

364
00:24:43,607 --> 00:24:45,669
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh man, what a ball club we had.

365
00:24:47,350 --> 00:24:48,251
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what it takes.

366
00:24:48,291 --> 00:24:49,752
[SPEAKER_00]: You've got to have enough balance.

367
00:24:50,072 --> 00:24:54,075
[SPEAKER_01]: Down in the street, was Clarence Craft playing that year?

368
00:24:54,095 --> 00:24:56,056
[SPEAKER_00]: Clarence Craft, I remember him very well.

369
00:24:56,076 --> 00:24:57,077
[SPEAKER_00]: He did a lot of home runs, didn't he?

370
00:24:57,097 --> 00:24:57,978
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, he did.

371
00:24:58,178 --> 00:25:00,079
[SPEAKER_00]: He was a great favorite in that league.

372
00:25:01,977 --> 00:25:07,400
[SPEAKER_00]: Back in the minor league days then, they had stars that the people just worshipped like they did in the major leagues.

373
00:25:07,940 --> 00:25:11,662
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, Ziggy Sears in the Texas league, man, he was some man down there.

374
00:25:12,122 --> 00:25:15,264
[SPEAKER_00]: Went on umpire for many years in the National League.

375
00:25:18,065 --> 00:25:23,568
[SPEAKER_01]: How about that, Toledo, you played in Swainfield?

376
00:25:23,728 --> 00:25:24,108
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

377
00:25:24,488 --> 00:25:25,449
[SPEAKER_01]: What was that like?

378
00:25:26,069 --> 00:25:28,650
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I'll tell you, I had a short right field fence.

379
00:25:29,711 --> 00:25:30,371
[SPEAKER_00]: Very short.

380
00:25:32,425 --> 00:25:33,866
[SPEAKER_00]: It was tough to pitch in that park.

381
00:25:34,666 --> 00:25:39,748
[SPEAKER_00]: I think the toughest pitcher I ever caught in my whole baseball career was Parmalee.

382
00:25:40,949 --> 00:25:42,169
[SPEAKER_00]: And he was the wildest.

383
00:25:42,229 --> 00:25:46,071
[SPEAKER_00]: I said he was the only fellow that could pitch one-third of a game under home plate.

384
00:25:46,091 --> 00:25:46,731
[SPEAKER_00]: He hit me all over.

385
00:25:46,751 --> 00:25:47,531
