778
00:49:04,559 --> 00:49:05,860
[SPEAKER_00]: He was a real guy.

779
00:49:07,556 --> 00:49:08,317
[SPEAKER_00]: Can he play ball?

780
00:49:09,398 --> 00:49:10,219
[SPEAKER_00]: Fairly good player.

781
00:49:10,880 --> 00:49:12,061
[SPEAKER_01]: He talked a lot.

782
00:49:12,782 --> 00:49:16,025
[SPEAKER_00]: He went to Chicago and led the league in talking.

783
00:49:16,806 --> 00:49:19,949
[SPEAKER_00]: But he was a hilarious fellow.

784
00:49:21,010 --> 00:49:22,571
[SPEAKER_00]: Some kind of a fighter too.

785
00:49:22,611 --> 00:49:24,793
[SPEAKER_00]: He used to fight some.

786
00:49:25,174 --> 00:49:27,256
[SPEAKER_00]: I used to see him a few fights down.

787
00:49:28,037 --> 00:49:29,678
[SPEAKER_00]: So I went to Waco, Texas one night.

788
00:49:29,698 --> 00:49:30,399
[SPEAKER_00]: We were over there.

789
00:49:37,138 --> 00:49:38,679
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, how about pitching to Ruth?

790
00:49:40,859 --> 00:49:49,043
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, some of our fellows handle Ruth pretty good, like Tommy Bridges and Schoolboy Rowe.

791
00:49:49,783 --> 00:49:52,644
[SPEAKER_00]: That type of pitcher could handle him, but the Babe is unpredictable.

792
00:49:52,744 --> 00:49:54,665
[SPEAKER_00]: If he got his pitch, it's goodbye.

793
00:49:55,865 --> 00:50:01,487
[SPEAKER_00]: The thing that was so interesting to me about Ruth as a hitter,

794
00:50:04,263 --> 00:50:05,904
[SPEAKER_00]: He could really hit left-hand pitching.

795
00:50:05,924 --> 00:50:08,766
[SPEAKER_00]: Now Whiteheel, we thought, was one of our better pitchers.

796
00:50:09,947 --> 00:50:11,988
[SPEAKER_00]: And I've seen him hit Whiteheel just like he owned him.

797
00:50:12,008 --> 00:50:16,251
[SPEAKER_00]: And that Whiteheel could break that ball over that curveball, like rolling it off the table.

798
00:50:16,952 --> 00:50:18,873
[SPEAKER_00]: They would step right in there and bang.

799
00:50:19,353 --> 00:50:26,018
[SPEAKER_00]: He could hit it just as good as... How would you call the game for the pitchers?

800
00:50:26,058 --> 00:50:27,599
[SPEAKER_01]: Or how would they call it for you?

801
00:50:27,639 --> 00:50:30,001
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you work that out?

802
00:50:30,441 --> 00:50:31,922
[SPEAKER_00]: They'd go with you most of the time.

803
00:50:32,667 --> 00:50:40,192
[SPEAKER_00]: Occasionally, you'd have a pitcher out there that he would want to go a certain way with a hitter, or you'd go with him.

804
00:50:40,292 --> 00:50:44,534
[SPEAKER_00]: I used to work with Whitey or Marbury or pitchers like that.

805
00:50:44,554 --> 00:50:51,579
[SPEAKER_00]: There'd be a certain time in the game, you get to a certain situation, and they had in their mind there was a pitch they wanted to throw.

806
00:50:52,339 --> 00:51:02,165
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I'd call for the pitch that I thought we should throw, but they'd look me off, and I knew what they were doing, and I'd give them the sign of the other because we wanted to shortstop in second base, but no one was coming.

807
00:51:03,690 --> 00:51:09,232
[SPEAKER_00]: But 90% of the time, the pitchers would go with the catcher.

808
00:51:09,252 --> 00:51:10,132
[SPEAKER_00]: They did with me.

809
00:51:12,453 --> 00:51:22,915
[SPEAKER_00]: But I've always thought it would have been a mistake on my part, or it's a mistake on any catcher's part, to get bullheaded and say, well, you're going to throw this, you're going to throw that.

810
00:51:22,975 --> 00:51:28,057
[SPEAKER_00]: I think, at times, a pitcher is more confident in another pitch.

811
00:51:28,317 --> 00:51:29,457
[SPEAKER_00]: And we don't know that.

812
00:51:29,677 --> 00:51:30,797
[SPEAKER_00]: And you should go with him.

813
00:51:32,972 --> 00:51:35,573
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, would you go over the pitches before each game?

814
00:51:35,593 --> 00:51:35,753
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yes.

815
00:51:35,773 --> 00:51:37,434
[SPEAKER_01]: You'd sit down with the whole team, I guess.

816
00:51:37,514 --> 00:51:37,814
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

817
00:51:37,854 --> 00:51:39,494
[SPEAKER_00]: We'd have a meeting in the clubhouse.

818
00:51:39,574 --> 00:51:40,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Most clubs do that.

819
00:51:40,775 --> 00:51:42,235
[SPEAKER_00]: I know the Brooklyn Club did.

820
00:51:42,715 --> 00:51:50,278
[SPEAKER_00]: A lot of times in Detroit, especially the first game of a series, you would go down the batting order.

821
00:51:50,598 --> 00:51:52,699
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, you'd go over all the hitters on the club.

822
00:51:54,019 --> 00:51:58,041
[SPEAKER_00]: And so many of them are high ball hitters or low ball hitters.

823
00:51:58,981 --> 00:52:01,762
[SPEAKER_00]: What we'd dwell on were the holes you could pitch to.

824
00:52:02,570 --> 00:52:07,911
[SPEAKER_00]: Like a fella might be tight up or down and away.

825
00:52:09,051 --> 00:52:13,072
[SPEAKER_00]: And some fellas are dead fastball hitters.

826
00:52:13,092 --> 00:52:16,273
[SPEAKER_00]: Example, Bing Miller was the best curveball hitter of all time.

827
00:52:16,293 --> 00:52:17,733
[SPEAKER_00]: We were unable to throw him a curveball.

828
00:52:17,753 --> 00:52:18,594
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd just wire him out.

829
00:52:19,334 --> 00:52:22,054
[SPEAKER_00]: And we'd pitch him tight, pitch him on his hands.

830
00:52:22,735 --> 00:52:28,436
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you watch these fellas play and hit, and you learn where the holes are.

831
00:52:30,370 --> 00:52:34,332
[SPEAKER_00]: And the holes were not very big over top-notch hitters in the major leagues.

832
00:52:34,832 --> 00:52:36,753
[SPEAKER_00]: You've got to draw that line real fine.

833
00:52:37,814 --> 00:52:43,297
[SPEAKER_00]: And there's where so many pitchers in the minor leagues had great stuff, but you couldn't pitch in the major leagues.

834
00:52:43,637 --> 00:52:45,458
[SPEAKER_00]: They couldn't draw the line fine enough.

835
00:52:46,038 --> 00:52:48,859
[SPEAKER_00]: Because as that hitter goes up, the holes get smaller.

836
00:52:49,940 --> 00:52:54,402
[SPEAKER_00]: And as the pitcher goes up, he can draw the line finer, so everything kind of stays even.

837
00:52:55,162 --> 00:52:58,024
[SPEAKER_00]: But looking at it in another light, if the pitcher

838
00:52:58,542 --> 00:53:03,784
[SPEAKER_00]: control is not fine enough, or the holes on the hitter is too big.

839
00:53:04,164 --> 00:53:06,785
[SPEAKER_00]: Look out, neither one of them is going to stay in the base.

840
00:53:07,125 --> 00:53:09,745
[SPEAKER_00]: The other guy won't let them.

841
00:53:11,746 --> 00:53:19,809
[SPEAKER_00]: We used to tell the fellas in the spring when we cut the roster after I got into scouting fields in Florida working with spring training.

842
00:53:19,829 --> 00:53:24,650
[SPEAKER_00]: We'd bring a young fella in and say, well look, we're going to have to send you to Montreal, say, or someplace.

843
00:53:24,970 --> 00:53:27,571
[SPEAKER_00]: You need more experience as a hitter.

844
00:53:28,245 --> 00:53:29,325
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd say, well, what's wrong?

845
00:53:29,385 --> 00:53:32,667
[SPEAKER_00]: I can hit you here just as well as the guys you got out on the field.

846
00:53:33,447 --> 00:53:35,288
[SPEAKER_00]: And why can't I stay?

847
00:53:35,708 --> 00:53:38,009
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, well, we don't really decide that.

848
00:53:39,310 --> 00:53:41,951
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the fellas on the other clubs that decide it.

849
00:53:42,431 --> 00:53:45,972
[SPEAKER_00]: They won't let you stay up here unless you're ready to stay up here.

850
00:53:46,032 --> 00:53:46,553
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's true.

851
00:53:47,713 --> 00:53:48,974
[SPEAKER_00]: Very nice way to put it, though.

852
00:53:48,994 --> 00:53:49,974
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the way you do it.

853
00:53:50,494 --> 00:53:52,735
[SPEAKER_00]: And then they begin to see the light and accept it.

854
00:53:52,795 --> 00:53:55,296
[SPEAKER_01]: But I don't blame the other kid.

855
00:53:55,376 --> 00:53:57,337
[SPEAKER_01]: I would stay up there, too, if I could.

856
00:53:58,478 --> 00:54:01,900
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a tough thing to tell him.

857
00:54:02,500 --> 00:54:05,222
[SPEAKER_01]: Tell me, what do you talk about when you go out and meet with a pitcher?

858
00:54:06,642 --> 00:54:13,946
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you again go out and refresh his memory on how we decided to pitch him.

859
00:54:14,006 --> 00:54:22,630
[SPEAKER_00]: Remember now, in our meeting, we're going to keep the ball tight on this ball, and we're going to play him to pull, or we'll go away from him.

860
00:54:22,790 --> 00:54:27,973
[SPEAKER_00]: And because your club's going to be playing around that way, we're going to make him hit in the other direction.

861
00:54:28,803 --> 00:54:30,084
[SPEAKER_00]: And you just impress upon them.

862
00:54:30,104 --> 00:54:31,324
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you've got to remember to do that.

863
00:54:32,244 --> 00:54:32,985
[SPEAKER_00]: Concentrate.

864
00:54:33,005 --> 00:54:34,245
[SPEAKER_00]: You've got to get this guy out.

865
00:54:34,525 --> 00:54:40,667
[SPEAKER_01]: Or maybe some situation has developed, which you probably haven't anticipated, and you need some on the spot.

866
00:54:40,827 --> 00:54:48,070
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, and a lot of times you go out, you can see a fellow that has begun to push the ball, and he's not really slinging the ball good.

867
00:54:48,090 --> 00:54:51,451
[SPEAKER_00]: You go out and say, look, come on now, sling that ball good.

868
00:54:51,471 --> 00:54:52,351
[SPEAKER_00]: You begin to push it.

869
00:54:53,291 --> 00:54:56,152
[SPEAKER_00]: And you tell him, because you pitch and run after the pitch.

870
00:54:56,433 --> 00:54:57,193
[SPEAKER_00]: I said, sure, sir.

871
00:54:58,104 --> 00:54:59,125
[SPEAKER_00]: getting darker tired.

872
00:55:00,125 --> 00:55:10,612
[SPEAKER_00]: And of course, the manager could see this from the bench, but a lot of times a fellow would, if he's a three-quarter pitcher overhand, he'd begin to drop down a little bit more towards the side of him.

873
00:55:10,632 --> 00:55:13,794
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to go out there and say, look, get back up over the top.

874
00:55:14,374 --> 00:55:15,555
[SPEAKER_00]: Your ball is flattened out.

875
00:55:15,575 --> 00:55:16,715
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not doing anything.

876
00:55:16,976 --> 00:55:18,136
[SPEAKER_00]: Get back up there.

877
00:55:18,336 --> 00:55:19,177
[SPEAKER_00]: Reach back and get it.

878
00:55:20,338 --> 00:55:23,059
[SPEAKER_00]: And same way with this curveball now.

879
00:55:23,720 --> 00:55:24,820
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't try to overpower it.

880
00:55:24,840 --> 00:55:26,241
[SPEAKER_00]: Just put more spin on it.

881
00:55:26,301 --> 00:55:27,302
[SPEAKER_00]: It'll break better for you.

882
00:55:27,804 --> 00:55:29,665
[SPEAKER_00]: things like that that you talk to them about.

883
00:55:29,685 --> 00:55:37,987
[SPEAKER_00]: And a lot of times, they get tense up out there, and a little conversation like that breaks the tension, and they settle and come back.

884
00:55:39,288 --> 00:55:40,928
[SPEAKER_00]: And that seems really important to our country.

885
00:55:40,988 --> 00:55:45,370
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you tell all the stories about how the catcher goes out and tells you, where are we going to eat dinner tonight?

886
00:55:45,410 --> 00:55:45,990
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

887
00:55:46,530 --> 00:55:51,272
[SPEAKER_00]: I went out to a pitcher one time, old David's neighbor in Detroit.

888
00:55:51,552 --> 00:55:54,473
[SPEAKER_00]: I think he had sort of been out on the town the night before.

889
00:55:54,493 --> 00:55:57,654
[SPEAKER_00]: I went out to talk to him, and I said, hey,

890
00:55:58,978 --> 00:56:01,419
[SPEAKER_00]: What in the world did you have to drink last night?

891
00:56:01,519 --> 00:56:02,880
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't get near you.

892
00:56:03,220 --> 00:56:12,864
[SPEAKER_00]: He turned around and went back to the plate.

893
00:56:13,204 --> 00:56:15,105
[SPEAKER_01]: You really study all the other hitters though, huh?

894
00:56:15,345 --> 00:56:15,665
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

895
00:56:16,426 --> 00:56:17,426
[SPEAKER_00]: They show you.

896
00:56:17,526 --> 00:56:22,428
[SPEAKER_00]: You catch against the club two or three times and you begin to learn.

897
00:56:22,949 --> 00:56:26,350
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you remember the balls that they hit real good and where they were thrown.

898
00:56:26,370 --> 00:56:28,491
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you keep a book or just keep it up here?

899
00:56:29,488 --> 00:56:31,009
[SPEAKER_00]: Just keep it in your head.

900
00:56:31,029 --> 00:56:33,931
[SPEAKER_00]: I never kept a book, but you remember these guys.

901
00:56:34,091 --> 00:56:43,457
[SPEAKER_00]: If they're going to beat you at a ball game, you better remember, but be careful that you stay away from their strength and try to stay with their weakness.

902
00:56:44,077 --> 00:56:45,578
[SPEAKER_00]: You never ever hit or never live.

903
00:56:45,598 --> 00:56:47,580
[SPEAKER_00]: You've got a weakness somewhere around that strike zone.

904
00:56:49,001 --> 00:56:50,942
[SPEAKER_00]: And you try to distribute it.

905
00:56:51,322 --> 00:56:55,725
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I mean, one of the smartest positions I think on the team to be a catcher.

906
00:56:56,478 --> 00:56:56,878
[SPEAKER_01]: It is.

907
00:56:56,998 --> 00:56:59,380
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to know more about what's going on.

908
00:57:00,541 --> 00:57:06,764
[SPEAKER_00]: Know more about what's going on, what your pitcher can do, and watch the players on the infield.

909
00:57:06,784 --> 00:57:09,126
[SPEAKER_00]: Some fellows will drift out of position.

910
00:57:09,166 --> 00:57:10,547
[SPEAKER_01]: You can help in that respect.

911
00:57:10,567 --> 00:57:14,729
[SPEAKER_01]: I think that's why catchers like Cochran could probably be managed well as a catcher.

912
00:57:15,009 --> 00:57:19,352
[SPEAKER_01]: But I don't see how somebody like Chris Peeker in center field could be managing the club very well out there.

913
00:57:20,073 --> 00:57:24,535
[SPEAKER_00]: We always felt like a fellow as many and close to the picture of nationally being a catcher himself.

914
00:57:24,555 --> 00:57:25,556
[SPEAKER_00]: I always thought catchers

915
00:57:26,489 --> 00:57:31,772
[SPEAKER_00]: I had a great opportunity to be good managers, but if they could handle me.

916
00:57:32,652 --> 00:57:34,253
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the main thing.

917
00:57:34,293 --> 00:57:47,260
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll tell you the year I managed Mr. Rickey's club in Fort Worth, I had a bunch of young kids just back from the war, and man, they wanted to play, and they had great ability.

918
00:57:48,280 --> 00:57:54,203
[SPEAKER_00]: So I started to have meetings at the clubhouse, and I never had so many questions thrown at me.

919
00:57:54,223 --> 00:57:55,704
[SPEAKER_00]: And I told them at the outset, and I looked.

920
00:57:56,365 --> 00:58:00,726
[SPEAKER_00]: If there's anything you don't understand about this game and you want to know, you ask me.

921
00:58:01,686 --> 00:58:04,387
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if I don't know the answer, I'll sure get it for you."

922
00:58:04,747 --> 00:58:07,068
[SPEAKER_00]: I said, I can get it from Mr. Rickey.

923
00:58:07,088 --> 00:58:10,108
[SPEAKER_00]: He's one of the smartest men the game has ever known.

924
00:58:10,228 --> 00:58:17,790
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, he analyzed everything and knew so well about every phase of the game.

925
00:58:20,411 --> 00:58:23,812
[SPEAKER_00]: We used to go down to spring training and he'd have all the managers in the front row.

926
00:58:24,192 --> 00:58:25,472
[SPEAKER_00]: Everyone would have a meeting for an hour.

927
00:58:26,203 --> 00:58:27,643
[SPEAKER_00]: put all the players in the background.

928
00:58:28,804 --> 00:58:47,729
[SPEAKER_00]: And he'd start out, maybe let's just name a few, he'd start out with hitting, bunting, catching, first base, second base, shortstop, third base, doing a course of spring training, cover every position, and tell every one of them how it should be played.

929
00:58:48,249 --> 00:58:54,671
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he'd go into, he wanted all the players to hear this, but then he'd talk to the manager and say,

930
00:58:55,887 --> 00:58:56,947
[SPEAKER_00]: Here's the situation.

931
00:58:57,928 --> 00:58:59,209
[SPEAKER_00]: When are you going to play the infield end?

932
00:58:59,229 --> 00:59:00,389
[SPEAKER_00]: When are you going to play it back?

933
00:59:01,329 --> 00:59:03,050
[SPEAKER_00]: And he had the answers for all this.

934
00:59:04,331 --> 00:59:06,652
[SPEAKER_00]: And it was good sound baseball.

935
00:59:06,712 --> 00:59:08,613
[SPEAKER_00]: And he'd give us all this.

936
00:59:09,373 --> 00:59:11,214
[SPEAKER_00]: And in the summer, he'd come and watch us play.

937
00:59:11,234 --> 00:59:18,977
[SPEAKER_00]: And you'd better remember what he said and not play your infield ends the wrong time and play it back and so forth.

938
00:59:19,638 --> 00:59:24,440
[SPEAKER_00]: So it was a great education, I'll tell you, of all the years I ever played professional ball.

939
00:59:25,151 --> 00:59:35,816
[SPEAKER_01]: When I got back into managing, I learned a great deal about baseball then that I didn't really get into too deep when I was playing.

940
00:59:35,876 --> 00:59:36,556
[SPEAKER_01]: I wish I had.

941
00:59:36,576 --> 00:59:37,957
[SPEAKER_01]: I wish I had this training.

942
00:59:38,697 --> 00:59:41,378
[SPEAKER_00]: I might have been able to contribute more.

943
00:59:42,699 --> 00:59:45,760
[SPEAKER_00]: But he had a great system.

944
00:59:45,780 --> 00:59:49,161
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why he was so successful with all of his clubs.

945
00:59:49,782 --> 00:59:52,243
[SPEAKER_00]: His players were, you look at that Dodger club.

946
00:59:53,538 --> 00:59:55,379
[SPEAKER_00]: to stay there so well at school.

947
00:59:56,579 --> 00:59:59,400
[SPEAKER_00]: And there's a way to do it.

948
00:59:59,440 --> 01:00:07,344
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a way to play every position and a way to pitch and a way to hit and bunt and all that sort of thing.

949
01:00:09,064 --> 01:00:15,027
[SPEAKER_00]: Baseball gets to be a vast field of things that you need to know.

950
01:00:15,107 --> 01:00:22,510
[SPEAKER_00]: I have a set of lectures here that were put together back in the early 50s when they had these coaches clinics all over the country.

951
01:00:23,627 --> 01:00:32,290
[SPEAKER_00]: And the National Association gave us permission to hand them out in coach meetings after I went to Milwaukee.

952
01:00:33,690 --> 01:00:35,691
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's a great set of lectures.

953
01:00:36,451 --> 01:00:37,572
[SPEAKER_00]: Most of them are 30 minutes.

954
01:00:37,592 --> 01:00:38,832
[SPEAKER_00]: Some of them are 45.

955
01:00:38,852 --> 01:00:41,513
[SPEAKER_00]: It covers every phase of the game.

956
01:00:42,693 --> 01:00:47,155
[SPEAKER_00]: And we'd give them out to college coaches, high school coaches.

957
01:00:47,175 --> 01:00:48,135
[SPEAKER_00]: Who delivered the lectures?

958
01:00:49,273 --> 01:00:50,374
[SPEAKER_00]: we were appointed.

959
01:00:50,594 --> 01:00:54,417
[SPEAKER_00]: In North Carolina, every year Mace Brown and I would do it.

960
01:00:54,437 --> 01:01:01,982
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd cover pitching and I'd cover catching and offensive and defensive plays.

961
01:01:02,843 --> 01:01:04,804
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the greatest thing that was ever put together.

962
01:01:04,824 --> 01:01:16,753
[SPEAKER_00]: Now this was put together by 15 or 20 of baseball's top players, like shortstop, second baseman, center fielder, catcher, first baseman,

963
01:01:17,798 --> 01:01:27,764
[SPEAKER_00]: And all by managers on percentage play and how to play your club and how to have spring training.

964
01:01:28,664 --> 01:01:31,986
[SPEAKER_00]: All these things, all in this set election, the finest thing I ever saw.

965
01:01:33,107 --> 01:01:36,709
[SPEAKER_00]: And we used them for years and years and give them out.

966
01:01:38,530 --> 01:01:39,711
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess they still do.

967
01:01:40,711 --> 01:01:41,832
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know what they do today.

968
01:01:41,872 --> 01:01:46,495
[SPEAKER_00]: I've been away from the game five years and I retired at the end of

969
01:01:49,760 --> 01:01:56,261
[SPEAKER_00]: I retired at age 68, 1972.

970
01:01:56,401 --> 01:02:07,124
[SPEAKER_00]: I had done so much special assignment scouting after I was director of scouting in Chicago and Milwaukee and Atlanta.

971
01:02:07,885 --> 01:02:10,285
[SPEAKER_00]: I went back into special assignment scouting.

972
01:02:10,945 --> 01:02:15,467
[SPEAKER_00]: I tell you, I was just flying all over the country for several years.

973
01:02:17,276 --> 01:02:24,221
[SPEAKER_00]: The next day I'd be here, and the next day in San Antonio, the next day in Los Angeles, the next day maybe in Spokane, wherever the top prospects were.

974
01:02:24,241 --> 01:02:26,423
[SPEAKER_01]: You didn't travel like that when you were in the minors, did you?

975
01:02:26,443 --> 01:02:26,763
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

976
01:02:27,023 --> 01:02:29,766
[SPEAKER_00]: What was train travel like in those early days?

977
01:02:30,426 --> 01:02:37,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I'll tell you, we rode trains on for years after I was in the Major Leagues, a long time before we began flying.

978
01:02:38,232 --> 01:02:40,093
[SPEAKER_00]: And all my career was on trains.

979
01:02:40,774 --> 01:02:41,895
[SPEAKER_01]: What did you do on the train?

980
01:02:42,735 --> 01:02:45,898
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it was just a group of fellas that all the

981
01:02:46,459 --> 01:02:54,325
[SPEAKER_00]: regular players were in the, what we called, number one car, and we always called number two the riffraff.

982
01:02:54,906 --> 01:02:57,808
[SPEAKER_00]: And they put the secondary players and pitchers back there.

983
01:02:58,689 --> 01:03:00,850
[SPEAKER_00]: And guys played a lot there.

984
01:03:01,050 --> 01:03:02,551
[SPEAKER_00]: They were like a bunch of kids.

985
01:03:03,372 --> 01:03:04,613
[SPEAKER_00]: And they had a good time.

986
01:03:06,374 --> 01:03:13,340
[SPEAKER_00]: They told me I went to the Texas League, would ride all night in the doggone trains with the windows up, didn't have any air conditioning.

987
01:03:13,760 --> 01:03:14,841
[SPEAKER_00]: Hot as whiz.

988
01:03:15,627 --> 01:03:18,628
[SPEAKER_00]: First thing he told me, he said, don't sleep over near the window.

989
01:03:18,648 --> 01:03:22,830
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, those mosquitoes will ram their bills right through there and get you right through the screen.

990
01:03:23,811 --> 01:03:25,832
[SPEAKER_00]: So, uh, uh.

991
01:03:26,352 --> 01:03:27,932
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't know how you could sleep in that situation.

992
01:03:27,953 --> 01:03:29,193
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I didn't sleep much.

993
01:03:29,253 --> 01:03:30,654
[SPEAKER_00]: You just rested a little bit.

994
01:03:30,674 --> 01:03:34,415
[SPEAKER_00]: You really couldn't sleep because, uh, it was so hot.

995
01:03:35,496 --> 01:03:38,437
[SPEAKER_00]: And later on, they began to come over with some air conditioning, but.

996
01:03:39,398 --> 01:03:41,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Leaving in the hotel room, it's been pretty hot.

997
01:03:41,479 --> 01:03:42,519
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it was hot.

998
01:03:43,386 --> 01:03:57,931
[SPEAKER_00]: Down at the Rice Hotel in Houston, we'd go in there in the Texas League, we'd go up to bed, we'd wet the sheet, and pull the bed out and have big ceiling fans right under the fan, wet that sheet, and we'd be pretty comfortable.

999
01:03:57,951 --> 01:04:01,972
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the most air-conditioned we could get a hold of in those days.

1000
01:04:02,852 --> 01:04:08,094
[SPEAKER_01]: But... What about playing on a hot Sunday afternoon in Houston?

1001
01:04:08,354 --> 01:04:11,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, sweet boy.

1002
01:04:11,275 --> 01:04:11,895
[SPEAKER_00]: Way cold.

1003
01:04:13,111 --> 01:04:14,691
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, Waco had an oil infield.

1004
01:04:14,711 --> 01:04:15,692
[SPEAKER_00]: You couldn't stand still.

1005
01:04:15,732 --> 01:04:19,473
[SPEAKER_00]: And I've seen the infield just stand out there like a prancing horse.

1006
01:04:19,493 --> 01:04:20,713
[SPEAKER_01]: They pour water in the shoes.

1007
01:04:20,733 --> 01:04:21,633
[SPEAKER_00]: The field was so hot.

1008
01:04:21,713 --> 01:04:24,094
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, they pour water in the shoes between innings.

1009
01:04:24,234 --> 01:04:26,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Actually, pour it right in there.

1010
01:04:26,094 --> 01:04:30,376
[SPEAKER_00]: It was so hot you could feel the spikes under there.

1011
01:04:30,416 --> 01:04:31,676
[SPEAKER_00]: Would anybody pass out?

1012
01:04:31,696 --> 01:04:35,017
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you'd get in shape.

1013
01:04:35,637 --> 01:04:38,458
[SPEAKER_00]: You could get yourself conditioned to that hot weather.

1014
01:04:38,638 --> 01:04:39,378
[SPEAKER_00]: You'd go out and play.

1015
01:04:40,580 --> 01:04:44,701
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it was hot, but... Did you ever catch a doubleheader in that kind of heat?

1016
01:04:44,741 --> 01:04:46,481
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

1017
01:04:46,501 --> 01:04:47,501
[SPEAKER_01]: How much weight did you lose?

1018
01:04:48,041 --> 01:04:52,142
[SPEAKER_01]: About eight, nine pounds, somewhere in that area in the afternoon.

1019
01:04:52,162 --> 01:04:53,302
[SPEAKER_00]: Did they have salt pills?

1020
01:04:53,362 --> 01:04:56,643
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, but you'd gain it back by the next day.

1021
01:04:57,583 --> 01:04:58,163
[SPEAKER_00]: Lose it again.

1022
01:04:58,623 --> 01:05:01,304
[SPEAKER_00]: Ty Cobb used to have a mix that he had put together.

1023
01:05:01,324 --> 01:05:02,384
[SPEAKER_00]: We'd play in Washington.

1024
01:05:02,444 --> 01:05:07,245
[SPEAKER_00]: It was awfully hot in Washington, especially with the rain in the morning and the clear off in the afternoon.

1025
01:05:08,777 --> 01:05:19,703
[SPEAKER_00]: You'd take a, have the trainer take a sack, fill it full of oatmeal, and put it in a bucket of ice water, and cut up a lot of lemons and oranges and put them in there.

1026
01:05:20,363 --> 01:05:21,244
[SPEAKER_00]: And you'd drink that.

1027
01:05:21,884 --> 01:05:24,245
[SPEAKER_01]: That was the most healthful drink you ever tasted.

1028
01:05:25,026 --> 01:05:26,206
[SPEAKER_00]: And it would strengthen you.

1029
01:05:26,286 --> 01:05:29,548
[SPEAKER_00]: It wouldn't make you sick like if you were to drink ice water.

1030
01:05:31,149 --> 01:05:33,690
[SPEAKER_01]: And a lot of used that back in the day.

1031
01:05:33,710 --> 01:05:37,332
[SPEAKER_01]: Now these players today, when they're fishing airplanes,

1032
01:05:38,558 --> 01:05:40,699
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know what it was, but it's so much nicer.

1033
01:05:40,759 --> 01:05:44,042
[SPEAKER_00]: I know when I got into scouting, I traveled a lot with the Cubs.

1034
01:05:44,162 --> 01:05:48,184
[SPEAKER_00]: I was in Chicago 13 years, Mr. Wrigley, in scouting.

1035
01:05:48,204 --> 01:05:50,826
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I did director scouting there.

1036
01:05:50,886 --> 01:05:54,088
[SPEAKER_00]: What years were those with the Cubs?

1037
01:05:54,208 --> 01:05:58,951
[SPEAKER_00]: I was there in, let's see, 47 to the end of 59.

1038
01:05:59,211 --> 01:06:06,096
[SPEAKER_00]: I scouted there, did all special assignment scouting all the time I was there.

1039
01:06:07,257 --> 01:06:09,398
[SPEAKER_00]: And until the last year I was director of scouting.

1040
01:06:09,418 --> 01:06:10,639
[SPEAKER_00]: And then I went to Milwaukee.

1041
01:06:11,679 --> 01:06:15,481
[SPEAKER_00]: And see, I spent nine years with Milwaukee.

1042
01:06:15,561 --> 01:06:17,382
[SPEAKER_00]: 60 to 60 through 68.

1043
01:06:17,622 --> 01:06:28,688
[SPEAKER_00]: I went to Milwaukee in the, well, the fall of, was there 60?

1044
01:06:40,894 --> 01:06:46,337
[SPEAKER_00]: through, let me figure it out now.

1045
01:06:48,119 --> 01:06:56,524
[SPEAKER_00]: I was there five years in Milwaukee, went to Atlanta, and stayed on there until the end of 70.

1046
01:06:58,285 --> 01:07:00,066
[SPEAKER_00]: And I went to Montreal in 71, 72, 73.

1047
01:07:00,086 --> 01:07:05,089
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, all my people moved from Atlanta to Montreal.

1048
01:07:05,850 --> 01:07:10,433
[SPEAKER_00]: And I went on up there with John McHale and scouted three more years of Special Assignments

1049
01:07:11,708 --> 01:07:13,690
[SPEAKER_00]: But I was director of scouting all the time.

1050
01:07:13,750 --> 01:07:18,073
[SPEAKER_00]: I was in Milwaukee from 1960 through 1966.

1051
01:07:22,317 --> 01:07:24,358
[SPEAKER_01]: And down to Atlanta, too, when you went to Atlanta?

1052
01:07:24,378 --> 01:07:25,459
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, I went to Atlanta.

1053
01:07:25,479 --> 01:07:27,901
[SPEAKER_00]: I remained director of scouting through 1966, 1967, 1968, 1969.

1054
01:07:27,961 --> 01:07:31,364
[SPEAKER_00]: I did special assignment scouting in Atlanta.

1055
01:07:42,612 --> 01:07:46,213
[SPEAKER_00]: And Jim Fanning took my place there as Director of Scouting.

1056
01:07:46,893 --> 01:07:55,735
[SPEAKER_00]: And then I moved from there to Montreal, continued in Special Assignment Scouting until I retired at the end of 72.

1057
01:07:55,935 --> 01:08:04,197
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, now getting back earlier here, you mentioned earlier in the Texas League you didn't have night ball.

1058
01:08:04,217 --> 01:08:05,517
[SPEAKER_01]: You played in the hot afternoons.

1059
01:08:06,017 --> 01:08:07,438
[SPEAKER_01]: When did you first play night ball?

1060
01:08:07,898 --> 01:08:12,519
[SPEAKER_01]: First night game I would play, it was in Cincinnati after I'd gone to Brooklyn.

1061
01:08:14,111 --> 01:08:16,112
[SPEAKER_00]: They didn't have any night ball ahead of that, you see.

1062
01:08:16,132 --> 01:08:17,572
[SPEAKER_00]: They were just bringing it in to Cincinnati.

1063
01:08:17,592 --> 01:08:19,333
[SPEAKER_01]: And you were in the Big Lakes from then on.

1064
01:08:19,353 --> 01:08:22,795
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess they went into the minors in about 30 or so, first night ball.

1065
01:08:22,835 --> 01:08:26,477
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, the first night game I think was played out in Des Moines.

1066
01:08:27,077 --> 01:08:34,321
[SPEAKER_00]: But I never did play in a night game until I actually went to the Cincinnati Park one night and played the first night game.

1067
01:08:34,361 --> 01:08:34,982
[SPEAKER_00]: The next big game?

1068
01:08:35,002 --> 01:08:36,823
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it was a regular league game.

1069
01:08:38,183 --> 01:08:39,384
[SPEAKER_01]: That was... Oh, when you were with...

1070
01:08:39,424 --> 01:08:40,805
[SPEAKER_01]: I was with Brooklyn.

1071
01:08:41,770 --> 01:08:43,230
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me finish off your career here.

1072
01:08:43,250 --> 01:08:45,891
[SPEAKER_01]: Detroit, you were from through 38?

1073
01:08:46,911 --> 01:08:49,931
[SPEAKER_00]: I went to Brooklyn in the latter part of the year.

1074
01:08:50,191 --> 01:08:53,352
[SPEAKER_00]: I'd say along about first of September.

1075
01:08:54,412 --> 01:08:55,072
[SPEAKER_01]: About 38?

1076
01:08:55,392 --> 01:08:55,752
[SPEAKER_00]: 38.

1077
01:08:56,092 --> 01:08:56,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

1078
01:08:56,433 --> 01:08:57,273
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, how long were you?

1079
01:08:57,493 --> 01:08:59,333
[SPEAKER_00]: I was in Brooklyn in 39.

1080
01:09:01,453 --> 01:09:07,594
[SPEAKER_00]: And I was traded to New York for Jimmy Ripple in the fall of 39.

1081
01:09:08,615 --> 01:09:10,375
[SPEAKER_00]: And I was in New York all of 40.

1082
01:09:12,647 --> 01:09:22,814
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't play any, but I was a bullpen coach and was an active player on the club, although I didn't play.

1083
01:09:22,834 --> 01:09:25,596
[SPEAKER_01]: Who was the catcher, Harry Danning?

1084
01:09:25,616 --> 01:09:28,838
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, Babe Phelps, Harry Danning.

1085
01:09:28,858 --> 01:09:32,341
[SPEAKER_00]: Danning was the catcher in New York, and Ken O'Day.

1086
01:09:33,722 --> 01:09:42,268
[SPEAKER_00]: But then I was released by New York at the end of the 1940 season, and I went to Milwaukee with Bill Killefer.

1087
01:09:44,652 --> 01:09:51,516
[SPEAKER_00]: and was made a free agent, and I signed with the St. Louis Browns in the spring of 42.

1088
01:09:51,596 --> 01:09:53,377
[SPEAKER_00]: You were with Milwaukee 41?

1089
01:09:53,917 --> 01:09:55,418
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

1090
01:09:55,558 --> 01:10:01,541
[SPEAKER_00]: In 42, I went to spring training with the St. Louis Browns.

1091
01:10:01,581 --> 01:10:10,466
[SPEAKER_00]: I stayed on with them until about the 1st of May, and I was released and signed by the St. Louis Cardinals.

1092
01:10:11,180 --> 01:10:18,225
[SPEAKER_00]: And I stayed there on the Cardinal Club, I guess for about, I'd say six weeks.

1093
01:10:19,245 --> 01:10:25,769
[SPEAKER_00]: And Mr. Rickey wanted me to go over and take over the Rochester Club and manage it for the rest of the years, playing manager.

1094
01:10:27,530 --> 01:10:35,215
[SPEAKER_00]: Which I did because Crabtree was a manager and he had, he took sick and he couldn't manage so he had to drop out.

1095
01:10:36,436 --> 01:10:39,118
[SPEAKER_00]: And through the winter,

1096
01:10:39,645 --> 01:10:49,250
[SPEAKER_00]: Then of 42, Mr. Rickley, Mr. Rickey went to Brooklyn at that time from St. Louis.

1097
01:10:49,850 --> 01:10:53,032
[SPEAKER_00]: So he immediately took me to Brooklyn with him.

1098
01:10:53,232 --> 01:11:01,396
[SPEAKER_00]: And I stayed in Brooklyn as an active player due to wartime years, 43, 44, and back in 45 until July of 45.

1099
01:11:11,538 --> 01:11:17,743
[SPEAKER_00]: put Sukuth and I off of the active list, and we began scouting.

1100
01:11:17,763 --> 01:11:18,684
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when we scouted.

1101
01:11:18,724 --> 01:11:20,985
[SPEAKER_00]: I helped scout all the colored clubs at that time.

1102
01:11:21,326 --> 01:11:24,608
[SPEAKER_00]: We got Newcomb, Campanella, and all those fellas.

1103
01:11:24,628 --> 01:11:26,990
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when we scouted.

1104
01:11:27,010 --> 01:11:28,711
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when those fellas came on.

1105
01:11:29,712 --> 01:11:33,875
[SPEAKER_00]: And he put Sukuth and I with George Sisler and Wynne Matthews.

1106
01:11:35,216 --> 01:11:40,000
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where we began learning about scouting.

1107
01:11:41,085 --> 01:11:49,612
[SPEAKER_00]: And I continued in scouting until my brief periods of managing in 46 and 47.

1108
01:11:50,973 --> 01:11:54,696
[SPEAKER_00]: And I continued on in scouting until I retired.

1109
01:11:54,816 --> 01:11:56,918
[SPEAKER_00]: 46 and 47 Fort Worth, right?

1110
01:11:57,198 --> 01:12:00,681
[SPEAKER_00]: I was 46 in Fort Worth and 47 in Macon, Georgia.

1111
01:12:00,701 --> 01:12:01,902
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when I went with the Cubs.

1112
01:12:03,784 --> 01:12:05,465
[SPEAKER_00]: Chicago Cubs, Jack Shinn.

1113
01:12:07,103 --> 01:12:11,285
[SPEAKER_00]: And I managed there, and at the end of the year, I went on their scouting staff.

1114
01:12:11,605 --> 01:12:13,726
[SPEAKER_00]: What did you feel about scouting for these black players?

1115
01:12:14,726 --> 01:12:15,086
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, right.

1116
01:12:15,126 --> 01:12:16,627
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll tell you that was an experience.

1117
01:12:16,647 --> 01:12:17,947
[SPEAKER_00]: We'd go to New York.

1118
01:12:18,888 --> 01:12:22,889
[SPEAKER_00]: They played a doubleheader every Sunday, but they had four colored clubs playing in there.

1119
01:12:23,510 --> 01:12:27,811
[SPEAKER_00]: So we got to see so many more players every Sunday afternoon.

1120
01:12:28,632 --> 01:12:35,395
[SPEAKER_00]: They'd play one week in the Yankee Stadium and the next week in Polo Ground, because one of the other clubs would be out of town.

1121
01:12:37,092 --> 01:12:40,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, Sessler and Sookie and Matthews and I, we'd all go over there.

1122
01:12:41,633 --> 01:12:43,473
[SPEAKER_00]: That was an experience to see.

1123
01:12:44,574 --> 01:12:47,014
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, it was attended by mostly colored people.

1124
01:12:48,074 --> 01:12:51,875
[SPEAKER_00]: And they danced up and down the aisles and threw stuff and bet money.

1125
01:12:52,055 --> 01:12:54,515
[SPEAKER_00]: And they had great players on the field.

1126
01:12:54,576 --> 01:12:56,176
[SPEAKER_00]: Look at the players we were looking at.

1127
01:12:57,256 --> 01:13:02,097
[SPEAKER_00]: And I remember old Josh Gibson in a game in the Yankee Stadium one Sunday afternoon.

1128
01:13:02,897 --> 01:13:07,038
[SPEAKER_00]: He hit a ball, I believe, to right field in his right-hand interval as far as Ruth would ever hit one.

1129
01:13:08,272 --> 01:13:16,197
[SPEAKER_01]: He was a powerhouse hitter.

1130
01:13:16,237 --> 01:13:25,384
[SPEAKER_00]: We got to see so many of the colored players that came on and made their way big in baseball.

1131
01:13:25,804 --> 01:13:28,366
[SPEAKER_00]: It was a free and open market then.

1132
01:13:28,466 --> 01:13:29,547
[SPEAKER_00]: It was up for grabs.

1133
01:13:29,567 --> 01:13:33,750
[SPEAKER_00]: So Mr. Rickey got in on the ground floor and scouted these players.

1134
01:13:35,328 --> 01:13:43,075
[SPEAKER_00]: When he got to Campanella, Jackie Robertson and Milcom just got top players in the colored field.

1135
01:13:43,855 --> 01:13:45,477
[SPEAKER_00]: And they were young then, you see.

1136
01:13:46,598 --> 01:13:52,523
[SPEAKER_00]: And he did extensive scouting, and he scouted colored players all down in Mexico and everywhere then.

1137
01:13:53,644 --> 01:14:00,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you ever think much about this when you were playing ball back in the 30s and 20s, about the fact that there were no blacks in it?

1138
01:14:00,109 --> 01:14:00,610
[SPEAKER_00]: Not a one.

1139
01:14:00,650 --> 01:14:03,252
[SPEAKER_00]: I never played against a colored player in my baseball career.

1140
01:14:04,017 --> 01:14:07,539
[SPEAKER_00]: came on after I finished and got into the scouting field.

1141
01:14:08,519 --> 01:14:12,441
[SPEAKER_00]: And I really scouted colored players from then on.

1142
01:14:13,201 --> 01:14:18,143
[SPEAKER_00]: I did this special assignment on Ernie Banks and we bought Ernie Banks from the Kansas City Monarchs.

1143
01:14:19,264 --> 01:14:21,185
[SPEAKER_00]: I was put on him by Whit Matthews.

1144
01:14:21,265 --> 01:14:33,270
[SPEAKER_00]: I was in Chicago and he called me in the office one morning and said, Ray, I want you to fly off to Davenport, Iowa and see a young colored shortstop that we just had a call about.

1145
01:14:34,008 --> 01:14:43,015
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, the business manager down in Macon saw that Kansas City Monarch Club play there last night, and the business manager's name was Gordon.

1146
01:14:43,756 --> 01:14:46,258
[SPEAKER_00]: And he says, somebody ought to see Banks.

1147
01:14:46,939 --> 01:14:48,560
[SPEAKER_00]: He's a fine-looking young ballplayer.

1148
01:14:49,501 --> 01:14:51,843
[SPEAKER_00]: So I immediately went out to Davenport and picked him up.

1149
01:14:53,164 --> 01:14:57,487
[SPEAKER_00]: And that night, Buck O'Neill was the manager of the Monarchs at that time.

1150
01:14:58,488 --> 01:15:00,790
[SPEAKER_00]: And I watched Banks play and take in the field.

1151
01:15:01,635 --> 01:15:06,538
[SPEAKER_00]: I called Buck O'Neill over and I told him who I was and I said, tell me something about this kid Banks.

1152
01:15:07,218 --> 01:15:09,119
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, well I'll tell you, he's a fine boy.

1153
01:15:09,139 --> 01:15:12,040
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd just come back from the service and he said he has good ability.

1154
01:15:13,521 --> 01:15:21,605
[SPEAKER_00]: So through us scouting and reporting and buying banks, it brought Buck O'Neill in Chicago.

1155
01:15:21,645 --> 01:15:25,347
[SPEAKER_00]: I bet you he stayed there 20 years scouting, just through banks.

1156
01:15:25,988 --> 01:15:30,370
[SPEAKER_00]: But I still have my original report right here now, my file that I made on banks.

1157
01:15:31,282 --> 01:15:33,864
[SPEAKER_00]: And we bought banks for $21,000.

1158
01:15:34,284 --> 01:15:46,994
[SPEAKER_00]: And about two or three years later, St. Louis offered Mr. Wrigley $500,000 for banks.

1159
01:15:47,074 --> 01:15:50,376
[SPEAKER_00]: And Mr. Wrigley says, I don't want $500,000 for banks.

1160
01:15:50,777 --> 01:15:52,198
[SPEAKER_00]: I just want another bank.

1161
01:15:53,479 --> 01:15:54,519
[SPEAKER_00]: He's not for sale.

1162
01:15:55,620 --> 01:15:58,562
[SPEAKER_00]: And yeah, we went on.

1163
01:15:59,003 --> 01:16:00,544
[SPEAKER_00]: I followed him on to Kansas City.

1164
01:16:01,069 --> 01:16:07,894
[SPEAKER_00]: and into Chicago and had the Negro American League Color World Series and they played in Chicago.

1165
01:16:09,295 --> 01:16:12,998
[SPEAKER_00]: So I met with Matthews at the White Sox Park that afternoon.

1166
01:16:13,038 --> 01:16:14,099
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, what about Banks?

1167
01:16:14,179 --> 01:16:17,221
[SPEAKER_00]: I said, get him, get him somewhere other.

1168
01:16:18,282 --> 01:16:19,863
[SPEAKER_00]: So the next morning I had a meeting with him.

1169
01:16:21,444 --> 01:16:28,489
[SPEAKER_00]: And I told Cabaretta, the manager at that time, I said, this boy is not too far away from playing in Chicago right now.

1170
01:16:29,062 --> 01:16:31,103
[SPEAKER_00]: And sure enough, the next year he played in Chicago.

1171
01:16:31,343 --> 01:16:34,104
[SPEAKER_00]: Did he have a good night that you saw him have the first night?

1172
01:16:34,124 --> 01:16:39,507
[SPEAKER_00]: He hit one in Davenport, over the fence, and there's a water tank way out there in the woods.

1173
01:16:39,527 --> 01:16:42,668
[SPEAKER_00]: And the last time I saw that ball, he was going by that water tank.

1174
01:16:42,748 --> 01:16:44,409
[SPEAKER_00]: I said, that's enough for me.

1175
01:16:45,630 --> 01:16:48,471
[SPEAKER_00]: If he's hit a ball that far one time, he can do it again.

1176
01:16:49,331 --> 01:16:50,732
[SPEAKER_01]: And he's in the Hall of Fame.

1177
01:16:50,952 --> 01:16:52,113
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, he was a great player.

1178
01:16:52,233 --> 01:16:54,274
[SPEAKER_00]: And a splendid fellow.

1179
01:16:55,734 --> 01:16:56,955
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought that was one of my

1180
01:16:59,045 --> 01:17:01,606
[SPEAKER_00]: top people that I ever signed.

1181
01:17:02,646 --> 01:17:05,607
[SPEAKER_00]: I flew out to the coast one year and signed Dick Ellsworth.

1182
01:17:06,527 --> 01:17:08,007
[SPEAKER_00]: So I'm playing a high school game.

1183
01:17:08,428 --> 01:17:12,649
[SPEAKER_00]: We got him and numerous other players that I was involved in.

1184
01:17:12,749 --> 01:17:20,511
[SPEAKER_00]: Too many to mention that we were signing in Detroit, I mean in Milwaukee and Atlanta when I was there.

1185
01:17:21,871 --> 01:17:28,493
[SPEAKER_01]: Tell me about, though back in the 20s if there is in your playing career,

1186
01:17:29,086 --> 01:17:30,846
[SPEAKER_01]: And there were no blacks playing ball.

1187
01:17:30,886 --> 01:17:35,167
[SPEAKER_01]: Did this occur to you fellows much who seemed... why they weren't there?

1188
01:17:35,208 --> 01:17:36,288
[SPEAKER_01]: You probably didn't think about it.

1189
01:17:36,368 --> 01:17:38,528
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, we knew there were some great players around.

1190
01:17:38,568 --> 01:17:43,810
[SPEAKER_00]: Because some of the boys would tell me of playing in exhibition games in the fall against them.

1191
01:17:43,870 --> 01:17:44,470
[SPEAKER_00]: I never did.

1192
01:17:44,490 --> 01:17:48,871
[SPEAKER_00]: But Tommy Bridges told me one time, he said, man, they really got some players.

1193
01:17:49,911 --> 01:17:54,732
[SPEAKER_00]: But they just hadn't broken the line to bring them into the major leagues.

1194
01:17:55,693 --> 01:17:57,633
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a pity, too, because there were a lot of great

1195
01:17:58,277 --> 01:18:03,960
[SPEAKER_00]: Colored players all along, even back before my time, they were great colored players.

1196
01:18:04,700 --> 01:18:08,282
[SPEAKER_00]: They just didn't recognize them and bring them into the game, which they should have done.

1197
01:18:08,342 --> 01:18:10,283
[SPEAKER_01]: There had been too much feeling against it, though.

1198
01:18:10,303 --> 01:18:11,384
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, there would have been then.

1199
01:18:11,424 --> 01:18:14,886
[SPEAKER_02]: And it was quite a daring thing for Ricky himself to do.

1200
01:18:15,006 --> 01:18:15,326
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

1201
01:18:15,506 --> 01:18:19,528
[SPEAKER_01]: And when he did, I guess there was some opposition to bringing Robinson in.

1202
01:18:19,548 --> 01:18:20,109
[SPEAKER_01]: A great deal.

1203
01:18:20,629 --> 01:18:21,850
[SPEAKER_00]: A great deal at that time.

1204
01:18:21,890 --> 01:18:28,133
[SPEAKER_00]: But the thing that hurt me more about the colored situation in baseball after I was in the scouting

1205
01:18:28,928 --> 01:18:31,910
[SPEAKER_00]: and traveling around, in Milwaukee for an example.

1206
01:18:33,611 --> 01:18:45,578
[SPEAKER_00]: Even as late as that has been, our club would go down to Houston and Aaron couldn't stay at the same hotel with us, he'd have to stay out of town someplace.

1207
01:18:45,958 --> 01:18:52,402
[SPEAKER_00]: And I'd see these young colored players down like in Blackwell, Oklahoma or someplace that we owned, that we'd signed.

1208
01:18:53,443 --> 01:18:56,845
[SPEAKER_00]: And they couldn't go into a restaurant and they had to stay out places.

1209
01:18:57,782 --> 01:19:02,466
[SPEAKER_00]: And it just didn't seem like people ought to be treated that way to me.

1210
01:19:03,708 --> 01:19:12,916
[SPEAKER_00]: And I was happy to see it change around and bring them in and give them the same accommodations that all the rest of the players were getting.

1211
01:19:14,377 --> 01:19:15,178
[SPEAKER_00]: And they were only right.

1212
01:19:15,959 --> 01:19:18,621
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess Robinson really had to put up with a lot at first.

1213
01:19:18,661 --> 01:19:19,322
[SPEAKER_01]: He sure did.

1214
01:19:20,162 --> 01:19:21,704
[SPEAKER_01]: But Rickey must have known his man.

1215
01:19:22,304 --> 01:19:22,785
[SPEAKER_00]: He did.

1216
01:19:25,078 --> 01:19:32,502
[SPEAKER_00]: He had great desire and had great ability and carried him over these rough spots and he just didn't let anything stop him.

1217
01:19:33,722 --> 01:19:37,745
[SPEAKER_00]: And he went right ahead and went about the business of playing baseball.

1218
01:19:40,986 --> 01:19:44,828
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, now here's something I like to ask old players.

1219
01:19:45,529 --> 01:19:46,749
[SPEAKER_01]: How'd you get along with umpires?

1220
01:19:47,790 --> 01:19:50,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I'll tell you, I got along pretty well with umpires.

1221
01:19:51,412 --> 01:19:54,013
[SPEAKER_00]: I had my usual, a few run-ins with them.

1222
01:19:56,378 --> 01:19:59,480
[SPEAKER_00]: Let me tell you a little incident that happened in Detroit one time.

1223
01:19:59,500 --> 01:20:00,141
[SPEAKER_00]: This is true.

1224
01:20:00,321 --> 01:20:01,702
[SPEAKER_00]: Bill Dineen was the umpire.

1225
01:20:03,463 --> 01:20:07,886
[SPEAKER_00]: We were going to have an off day, and we were playing that afternoon, and I heard the umpires talking.

1226
01:20:07,906 --> 01:20:14,151
[SPEAKER_00]: They said, well, what we'll do tomorrow, let's just take a little trip over to Windsor, visit around over there.

1227
01:20:14,171 --> 01:20:17,133
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you know, back in those days, it was dry in Detroit.

1228
01:20:17,253 --> 01:20:20,135
[SPEAKER_00]: You couldn't get beer or anything you wanted over in Canada.

1229
01:20:20,976 --> 01:20:23,257
[SPEAKER_00]: So the next day was an off day.

1230
01:20:23,277 --> 01:20:24,378
[SPEAKER_00]: The next day around,

1231
01:20:25,443 --> 01:20:27,124
[SPEAKER_00]: Deneen was umpiring back of the plate.

1232
01:20:28,085 --> 01:20:29,746
[SPEAKER_00]: And I thought he missed a strike or two.

1233
01:20:30,427 --> 01:20:37,953
[SPEAKER_00]: And I didn't turn around, I just backed up along the side of him and I said, you fellas must have got ahold of some bad stuff over in Canada yesterday.

1234
01:20:37,973 --> 01:20:40,555
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, that's all for you, get out of here.

1235
01:20:41,316 --> 01:20:46,740
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you know, he probably didn't, but I just, it's the meanest thing I could think of to say to him.

1236
01:20:47,280 --> 01:20:49,102
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, get out of here, you're through.

1237
01:20:49,702 --> 01:20:50,603
[SPEAKER_00]: So Bucky Harris

1238
01:20:50,992 --> 01:20:55,615
[SPEAKER_00]: bounced off the bench and started to play, and he says, what in the world are you throwing Ray out of the game for?

1239
01:20:56,255 --> 01:20:58,357
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, and you go with him.

1240
01:20:59,057 --> 01:21:00,958
[SPEAKER_00]: He's truly about to.

1241
01:21:01,559 --> 01:21:04,921
[SPEAKER_01]: So I really touched a nerve with him there.

1242
01:21:05,081 --> 01:21:11,545
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know to this day if the man ever had a bottle of beer or not, but I just couldn't think of anything else mean to say.

1243
01:21:11,565 --> 01:21:14,487
[SPEAKER_00]: Another time I had to run in with the umpire.

1244
01:21:16,868 --> 01:21:19,330
[SPEAKER_00]: He was having a real bad night.

1245
01:21:20,372 --> 01:21:25,455
[SPEAKER_00]: And I backed up alongside of him and I said, you sure you feel all right?

1246
01:21:25,475 --> 01:21:26,516
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, yes, I do.

1247
01:21:26,556 --> 01:21:30,118
[SPEAKER_00]: And I said, you just can't be that lousy and feel all right.

1248
01:21:30,178 --> 01:21:31,959
[SPEAKER_00]: I said, you've got to be sick.

1249
01:21:33,059 --> 01:21:33,940
[SPEAKER_00]: Get out of here.

1250
01:21:34,120 --> 01:21:34,640
[SPEAKER_00]: Who is that?

1251
01:21:34,660 --> 01:21:39,023
[SPEAKER_00]: It was a fellow down at Bacon, Georgia one night.

1252
01:21:39,543 --> 01:21:47,348
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know if he remembers his name now, but I'll tell you right now, when I told him that, I knew I was going to get the rest of the night off.

1253
01:21:48,417 --> 01:21:49,618
[SPEAKER_01]: That was a good line, though.

1254
01:21:49,658 --> 01:21:50,499
[SPEAKER_01]: Too good to waste.

1255
01:21:50,659 --> 01:21:51,440
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, sir.

1256
01:21:51,480 --> 01:21:55,723
[SPEAKER_00]: But I have gotten after umpires on those hot days.

1257
01:21:56,564 --> 01:21:58,245
[SPEAKER_00]: And right away, they sense it, you know.

1258
01:21:58,285 --> 01:22:01,548
[SPEAKER_00]: You're irritable, and you think they missed a strike or two.

1259
01:22:01,628 --> 01:22:07,773
[SPEAKER_00]: And they say, you can talk all you want to, but you're not going to be thrown out of this game.

1260
01:22:07,853 --> 01:22:10,095
[SPEAKER_00]: You're going to stay here and play.

1261
01:22:10,775 --> 01:22:12,957
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not going to get out of here on this hot day.

1262
01:22:12,977 --> 01:22:15,579
[SPEAKER_00]: And that hurt me, too, you know.

1263
01:22:17,527 --> 01:22:18,667
[SPEAKER_00]: You had to do something.

1264
01:22:19,047 --> 01:22:22,768
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean... Would you actually try to get thrown out sometimes?

1265
01:22:22,928 --> 01:22:23,108
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

1266
01:22:23,148 --> 01:22:25,129
[SPEAKER_00]: I just say these mean things to them.

1267
01:22:25,709 --> 01:22:26,589
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought they were mean.

1268
01:22:27,489 --> 01:22:29,990
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe they'd do a little better or bear down harder.

1269
01:22:30,010 --> 01:22:35,851
[SPEAKER_00]: I know now that when I sit here and talk that they were doing the best they could.

1270
01:22:36,411 --> 01:22:37,371
[SPEAKER_00]: Pretty tough job.

1271
01:22:37,731 --> 01:22:39,172
[SPEAKER_00]: It is a tough job.

1272
01:22:39,612 --> 01:22:45,633
[SPEAKER_00]: There's so many of those balls in the pits that are just right there on the corner that could be called either way.

1273
01:22:47,010 --> 01:22:51,834
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you tell if a ball is a sixteenth of an inch off of the plate or on the plate?

1274
01:22:52,855 --> 01:22:56,958
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a matter of seeing and judgment that you do these things.

1275
01:22:57,098 --> 01:23:01,582
[SPEAKER_00]: And I think the umpire does.

1276
01:23:02,042 --> 01:23:03,663
[SPEAKER_00]: I really admire umpires.

1277
01:23:03,803 --> 01:23:10,709
[SPEAKER_00]: I think it's a tough profession, but somebody must do it.

1278
01:23:11,309 --> 01:23:16,473
[SPEAKER_00]: And these fellows have got the training, and I think overall they do a wonderful job.

1279
01:23:17,142 --> 01:23:29,253
[SPEAKER_01]: You think, say you've got a batter up there who has a reputation for a very good eye, and he takes a pitch right on the outside corner.

1280
01:23:30,414 --> 01:23:33,116
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there a tendency you think the umpire might give him that?

1281
01:23:33,597 --> 01:23:41,724
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I think some of the little top hitters, they didn't seem to call him quite as, I don't think they called some of those fine liners as much as they did all day.

1282
01:23:43,625 --> 01:23:47,008
[SPEAKER_01]: Like Ty Cobb, who had such a good eye.

1283
01:23:47,548 --> 01:23:48,749
[SPEAKER_01]: He probably got a few breaks.

1284
01:23:49,649 --> 01:23:53,552
[SPEAKER_00]: Ted Williams had one of the greatest eyes at the plate of anybody.

1285
01:23:53,572 --> 01:23:55,914
[SPEAKER_00]: He could just take a ball, he wouldn't even offer it.

1286
01:23:55,974 --> 01:23:57,815
[SPEAKER_00]: It just barely missed that plate.

1287
01:23:57,835 --> 01:23:58,596
[SPEAKER_00]: I couldn't do that.

1288
01:23:59,036 --> 01:24:01,358
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe it did miss the plate, but the umpire said it didn't.

1289
01:24:01,438 --> 01:24:06,562
[SPEAKER_00]: If it was that close, I'd either swing or be called out, but he wouldn't even offer the ball.

1290
01:24:06,622 --> 01:24:07,803
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd just look at that ball.

1291
01:24:08,943 --> 01:24:09,924
[SPEAKER_00]: Great eyesight, man.

1292
01:24:09,944 --> 01:24:11,185
[SPEAKER_01]: That's really such a great hitter.

1293
01:24:13,270 --> 01:24:16,712
[SPEAKER_01]: Do any umpires stand out as being a little bit better?

1294
01:24:16,732 --> 01:24:20,874
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, there were so many good umpires over the years.

1295
01:24:20,894 --> 01:24:21,134
[SPEAKER_00]: Or worse.

1296
01:24:22,115 --> 01:24:28,458
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, there were some that maybe didn't rate as high as the others, but I'll tell you, there were some of those fellows that were really good.

1297
01:24:28,778 --> 01:24:31,940
[SPEAKER_00]: I always admired Cal Hubbard.

1298
01:24:32,060 --> 01:24:43,186
[SPEAKER_00]: One time, Lou Kline told me that the thing that hurt him so much in baseball is that he'd hit a ground ball down at the infield, and he'd run his tail off the first base and just get nicked.

1299
01:24:43,890 --> 01:24:56,517
[SPEAKER_00]: and said, instead of the umpire calling him out like they do today, he said, old Cal was standing down there with his hands in his vest and said, as he'd go by and take a look at it, he said, all he'd give him was just his thumb, like that.

1300
01:24:56,537 --> 01:24:57,618
[SPEAKER_00]: Just that little thumb.

1301
01:24:58,258 --> 01:25:02,981
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, one day he turned around to him and he said, can't you give me a better out than that?

1302
01:25:03,901 --> 01:25:04,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Lou Kline?

1303
01:25:04,582 --> 01:25:04,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1304
01:25:05,102 --> 01:25:06,623
[SPEAKER_00]: The infielder with the Cardinals?

1305
01:25:06,683 --> 01:25:09,164
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, he was later with the Cubs.

1306
01:25:10,065 --> 01:25:13,387
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, can't you give me a better out than that?

1307
01:25:14,299 --> 01:25:16,320
[SPEAKER_01]: Not many people argue with Cowley.

1308
01:25:16,800 --> 01:25:18,841
[SPEAKER_01]: Not many.

1309
01:25:18,901 --> 01:25:21,401
[SPEAKER_00]: He was a good umpire.

1310
01:25:21,441 --> 01:25:22,062
[SPEAKER_00]: Bill McGowan?

1311
01:25:22,482 --> 01:25:23,742
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I like Bill.

1312
01:25:23,822 --> 01:25:26,083
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember him in 34 and 5.

1313
01:25:26,903 --> 01:25:28,964
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, McGowan was a good umpire.

1314
01:25:29,324 --> 01:25:29,904
[SPEAKER_01]: Billy Evans?

1315
01:25:29,924 --> 01:25:31,325
[SPEAKER_01]: Billy Evans was a great umpire.

1316
01:25:31,345 --> 01:25:33,545
[SPEAKER_01]: Was he in business when you were growing up?

1317
01:25:33,565 --> 01:25:35,306
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, he was still umpiring.

1318
01:25:36,066 --> 01:25:39,687
[SPEAKER_01]: A lot of those fellas back there were real good umpires.

1319
01:25:39,827 --> 01:25:41,268
[SPEAKER_01]: What was it like in the World Series?

1320
01:25:42,339 --> 01:25:46,660
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, believe it or not, World Series, you can play it for years and years.

1321
01:25:46,741 --> 01:25:50,622
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just something about a World Series that's a little different.

1322
01:25:51,742 --> 01:25:53,983
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you get up to it more.

1323
01:25:54,243 --> 01:25:57,684
[SPEAKER_00]: There's more excitement.

1324
01:25:58,845 --> 01:26:05,027
[SPEAKER_00]: The idea of World Series, it is, it's different playing a regular season.

1325
01:26:05,227 --> 01:26:06,107
[SPEAKER_00]: There's something about it.

1326
01:26:06,307 --> 01:26:11,689
[SPEAKER_00]: And all the players feel a sense that it's extra special when you're in a World Series.

1327
01:26:15,847 --> 01:26:17,387
[SPEAKER_00]: But you didn't catch it either once?

1328
01:26:17,848 --> 01:26:21,090
[SPEAKER_00]: I finished up the last game of the 34 series.

1329
01:26:22,030 --> 01:26:26,873
[SPEAKER_00]: And Caulkin caught all the 35 series, because he won six games, and we won over the Cubs.

1330
01:26:27,994 --> 01:26:29,675
[SPEAKER_00]: And he had a great series.

1331
01:26:31,756 --> 01:26:33,817
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's too bad you couldn't have gotten in there.

1332
01:26:34,637 --> 01:26:34,938
[SPEAKER_01]: Well.

1333
01:26:35,218 --> 01:26:38,180
[SPEAKER_01]: And as you say, you're on a championship team, so.

1334
01:26:38,200 --> 01:26:39,860
[SPEAKER_00]: Great to win, I'll tell you.

1335
01:26:40,121 --> 01:26:42,382
[SPEAKER_00]: People have said so many times over the years,

1336
01:26:44,742 --> 01:26:46,003
[SPEAKER_00]: Why didn't you complain?

1337
01:26:46,043 --> 01:26:48,104
[SPEAKER_00]: Why didn't you get them to trade you and all?

1338
01:26:48,485 --> 01:27:04,615
[SPEAKER_00]: Like I said earlier in our talks, that I'd rather have been there and been second string than have been first string in some place where I'd worked myself to death and wouldn't have reaped the harvest that I did reap, money-wise, from being in Detroit.

1339
01:27:06,497 --> 01:27:08,358
[SPEAKER_01]: Did that team have, say, 34?

1340
01:27:08,378 --> 01:27:11,080
[SPEAKER_01]: Was there a leader on the field other than Cochran?

1341
01:27:12,435 --> 01:27:21,278
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, Cochran was really our leader, but we had men on there like Gaussman and Gerringer and... Billy Rogel, would he be a leader type?

1342
01:27:21,298 --> 01:27:23,839
[SPEAKER_00]: Not as much as some of the other fellas.

1343
01:27:25,959 --> 01:27:27,200
[SPEAKER_00]: I have to think that Cochran...

1344
01:27:27,280 --> 01:27:29,240
[SPEAKER_00]: I wouldn't think Gerringer, he's so quiet.

1345
01:27:29,280 --> 01:27:36,302
[SPEAKER_00]: Gerringer's quiet, but he did so many things so well, so easy that everybody was kind of... Well, it followed him, you know.

1346
01:27:39,754 --> 01:27:43,157
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, our holler guys were Gerald Walker and fellows like that.

1347
01:27:43,677 --> 01:27:44,538
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, he was something.

1348
01:27:45,759 --> 01:27:49,041
[SPEAKER_01]: Did he really swallow a bug in the back of trying to steal second base one time?

1349
01:27:49,161 --> 01:27:50,622
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you ever hear that story?

1350
01:27:50,662 --> 01:27:50,943
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1351
01:27:51,123 --> 01:28:02,852
[SPEAKER_00]: Jojo White one day, they had a close play at the plate, and he's sitting there, and he said he just placed his finger in his nose, and when he said, slide, he said he almost ratted his finger out the top of his head.

1352
01:28:07,406 --> 01:28:10,728
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, Gerald swallowed his tobacco one day.

1353
01:28:11,809 --> 01:28:15,091
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, he was a wild kind of a bass rocker.

1354
01:28:15,351 --> 01:28:16,412
[SPEAKER_00]: Unpredictable.

1355
01:28:17,133 --> 01:28:18,253
[SPEAKER_00]: Stole a lot of basses, too.

1356
01:28:18,273 --> 01:28:20,255
[SPEAKER_01]: Was he on his own, still?

1357
01:28:20,295 --> 01:28:21,395
[SPEAKER_00]: He might as well have been.

1358
01:28:21,495 --> 01:28:23,377
[SPEAKER_01]: He took everything in his own hands.

1359
01:28:24,217 --> 01:28:30,401
[SPEAKER_00]: He used to drive a car crazy, but he still had so much ability that he offset a lot of his mistakes.

1360
01:28:31,762 --> 01:28:32,623
[SPEAKER_00]: Stuff like that.

1361
01:28:34,714 --> 01:28:36,255
[SPEAKER_00]: Harvey Walker was up, too, for a while.

1362
01:28:36,275 --> 01:28:38,077
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, he was there a while, and he left.

1363
01:28:38,097 --> 01:28:39,198
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't stay too long.

1364
01:28:39,998 --> 01:28:43,962
[SPEAKER_00]: He was not quite the player that his brother was.

1365
01:28:44,182 --> 01:28:45,223
[SPEAKER_01]: How about Roy Johnson?

1366
01:28:45,243 --> 01:28:46,424
[SPEAKER_01]: Is he still with you?

1367
01:28:46,484 --> 01:28:48,165
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, we were with Roy.

1368
01:28:48,225 --> 01:28:50,767
[SPEAKER_00]: Roy was there and played quite a bit.

1369
01:28:52,509 --> 01:28:55,652
[SPEAKER_00]: He was sort of a temperamental type player.

1370
01:28:56,752 --> 01:28:58,254
[SPEAKER_00]: Still, he had good ability.

1371
01:29:01,476 --> 01:29:03,378
[SPEAKER_00]: But what about Billy Rogel?

1372
01:29:04,026 --> 01:29:06,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Billy was a good, steady shortstop.

1373
01:29:07,169 --> 01:29:14,275
[SPEAKER_00]: He was the kind of fellow that Cochran would pride him a little bit every once in a while and keep him on his toe and keep him going.

1374
01:29:15,036 --> 01:29:17,158
[SPEAKER_00]: He always had that plug at the back in his mind, didn't he?

1375
01:29:17,178 --> 01:29:18,399
[SPEAKER_00]: He was a good playmaker.

1376
01:29:18,419 --> 01:29:19,960
[SPEAKER_00]: He could really handle that glove.

1377
01:29:20,641 --> 01:29:24,144
[SPEAKER_00]: Covered a lot of ground and teamed with Garinger.

1378
01:29:24,164 --> 01:29:26,206
[SPEAKER_00]: They made a great combination of second base.

1379
01:29:27,187 --> 01:29:27,908
[SPEAKER_00]: And Marv Owen.

1380
01:29:28,108 --> 01:29:28,768
[SPEAKER_00]: Marv Owen.

1381
01:29:28,868 --> 01:29:30,470
[SPEAKER_00]: We used to call him Shovelhands.

1382
01:29:31,408 --> 01:29:31,908
[SPEAKER_00]: Shovelhands.

1383
01:29:31,948 --> 01:29:32,649
[SPEAKER_00]: Shovelhands.

1384
01:29:32,729 --> 01:29:35,189
[SPEAKER_00]: No matter what went down there, he scooped it up.

1385
01:29:35,229 --> 01:29:36,650
[SPEAKER_00]: He was like this.

1386
01:29:36,690 --> 01:29:39,051
[SPEAKER_00]: He had a head and a little bigger butt.

1387
01:29:39,471 --> 01:29:42,432
[SPEAKER_00]: And he was a sure-handed third baseman.

1388
01:29:43,092 --> 01:29:44,373
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't get beat up all over.

1389
01:29:44,413 --> 01:29:46,093
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd just catch it and throw him out.

1390
01:29:47,254 --> 01:29:59,138
[SPEAKER_00]: And you know, in the 35 series, when Greenberg got that fractured wrist, he moved right over to first base and did a whale of a job at first base and moved Fleet Clifton off the bench at third base.

1391
01:30:00,156 --> 01:30:07,160
[SPEAKER_00]: He got hit right here and fractured a bone in his elbow, in his wrist.

1392
01:30:08,341 --> 01:30:10,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Had Owen played first base before?

1393
01:30:10,902 --> 01:30:12,423
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, he had earlier in his career.

1394
01:30:12,443 --> 01:30:13,744
[SPEAKER_00]: He hadn't played in Detroit.

1395
01:30:13,784 --> 01:30:18,327
[SPEAKER_00]: He moved over there and he had such good hands, he played well over first base for us.

1396
01:30:21,068 --> 01:30:23,149
[SPEAKER_00]: But our clubs then had great depth.

1397
01:30:23,269 --> 01:30:24,870
[SPEAKER_00]: We had good bench strength.

1398
01:30:28,012 --> 01:30:28,372
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1399
01:30:28,833 --> 01:30:29,573
[SPEAKER_00]: Goose Gatlin.

1400
01:30:31,340 --> 01:30:35,544
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember that Goose's base hit in that 35th series.

1401
01:30:35,664 --> 01:30:41,749
[SPEAKER_00]: We saw Texas League signals right over second base that scored Cochran for the winning run in that sixth game.

1402
01:30:43,411 --> 01:30:45,232
[SPEAKER_00]: Man, I'd never seen a town go so wild.

1403
01:30:45,273 --> 01:30:48,015
[SPEAKER_00]: It was about tore down the ballpark in downtown, too.

1404
01:30:49,076 --> 01:30:55,362
[SPEAKER_00]: It was a great celebration for them to win that thing.

1405
01:30:55,962 --> 01:30:57,824
[SPEAKER_00]: They said it was a bigger celebration for them.

1406
01:30:58,340 --> 01:30:59,521
[SPEAKER_00]: World War I ended.

1407
01:31:00,261 --> 01:31:03,063
[SPEAKER_01]: Was he a very talkative chap, the goose?

1408
01:31:04,604 --> 01:31:05,364
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, ordinary.

1409
01:31:05,445 --> 01:31:07,686
[SPEAKER_00]: He, I mean, he's not overly talkative.

1410
01:31:09,167 --> 01:31:11,849
[SPEAKER_00]: Goose is a very likable fellow.

1411
01:31:11,869 --> 01:31:14,110
[SPEAKER_00]: He, uh...

1412
01:31:14,190 --> 01:31:17,132
[SPEAKER_00]: He, uh... Great admirer of my boys.

1413
01:31:17,692 --> 01:31:18,573
[SPEAKER_00]: My kids were young.

1414
01:31:19,213 --> 01:31:20,114
[SPEAKER_00]: They loved Goose.

1415
01:31:20,154 --> 01:31:22,255
[SPEAKER_00]: They just thought he was great.

1416
01:31:25,417 --> 01:31:26,678
[SPEAKER_00]: You could sure hit that ball.

1417
01:31:27,962 --> 01:31:28,923
[SPEAKER_00]: he really could hit.

1418
01:31:29,503 --> 01:31:30,744
[SPEAKER_01]: Tell me, did you ever catch a no-hitter?

1419
01:31:32,065 --> 01:31:39,690
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I caught a perfect game one time, oh yes, but I never caught a, eventually I didn't.

1420
01:31:41,651 --> 01:31:47,195
[SPEAKER_00]: I caught the game in Detroit that Tommy Bridges pitched against Washington.

1421
01:31:47,235 --> 01:31:52,458
[SPEAKER_00]: We went down to the ninth inning with two men out, and he had a perfect game.

1422
01:31:53,399 --> 01:31:55,700
[SPEAKER_00]: Hadn't walked a guy, didn't have a base hit.

1423
01:31:57,274 --> 01:31:59,516
[SPEAKER_00]: Walter Johnson was managing the Washington Club.

1424
01:32:00,876 --> 01:32:04,219
[SPEAKER_00]: And he brought in Dave Harris to pinch hit.

1425
01:32:05,760 --> 01:32:10,082
[SPEAKER_00]: With Judge, Joe Judge sitting on the bench and I believe Sam Rice both.

1426
01:32:10,423 --> 01:32:11,463
[SPEAKER_00]: And both of them left handed.

1427
01:32:11,483 --> 01:32:12,384
[SPEAKER_00]: But he put him up to hit.

1428
01:32:14,365 --> 01:32:16,587
[SPEAKER_00]: Tommy threw him a high fastball.

1429
01:32:16,607 --> 01:32:17,207
[SPEAKER_00]: It was about there.

1430
01:32:17,227 --> 01:32:18,488
[SPEAKER_00]: It was not even the strike zone.

1431
01:32:18,548 --> 01:32:19,609
[SPEAKER_00]: And he broke his back.

1432
01:32:19,629 --> 01:32:21,090
[SPEAKER_00]: He had his left heel pervasive.

1433
01:32:22,291 --> 01:32:23,892
[SPEAKER_01]: And spoiled that perfect game.

1434
01:32:24,192 --> 01:32:25,913
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you think he brought in Harris just to

1435
01:32:28,535 --> 01:32:32,597
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know, but Johnson sent him in there, which, ordinarily, you'd say, well, this is great.

1436
01:32:33,638 --> 01:32:35,759
[SPEAKER_00]: But he just took a wild swing at that first pitch.

1437
01:32:35,779 --> 01:32:41,103
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why he didn't send in Rice or Judge, because he wanted him to get the no-hitter.

1438
01:32:41,143 --> 01:32:42,003
[SPEAKER_01]: Harris was a good hitter.

1439
01:32:43,204 --> 01:32:47,527
[SPEAKER_00]: But he took a wild swing at this high fastball, and darned if he didn't break his head.

1440
01:32:47,547 --> 01:32:48,387
[SPEAKER_00]: Sounded like an experience.

1441
01:32:48,407 --> 01:32:56,132
[SPEAKER_00]: Get it in the field for a base hit, and just everybody died with it, because he was so close to a perfect game.

1442
01:32:57,326 --> 01:32:59,807
[SPEAKER_00]: But they didn't make it.

1443
01:33:02,509 --> 01:33:03,429
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you get the next minute?

1444
01:33:03,469 --> 01:33:03,549
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

1445
01:33:03,869 --> 01:33:05,730
[SPEAKER_01]: But you did catch a no-hitter in the minors, huh?

1446
01:33:05,750 --> 01:33:06,211
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yes.

1447
01:33:06,311 --> 01:33:06,851
[SPEAKER_00]: Where was this?

1448
01:33:06,931 --> 01:33:07,451
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know.

1449
01:33:07,471 --> 01:33:07,951
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's see.

1450
01:33:08,011 --> 01:33:12,434
[SPEAKER_00]: I think someplace I caught a game.

1451
01:33:12,514 --> 01:33:16,516
[SPEAKER_00]: I think it was when I was with the Toledo Club.

1452
01:33:16,716 --> 01:33:18,677
[SPEAKER_00]: It seemed like somebody pitched a no-hitter.

1453
01:33:19,938 --> 01:33:21,558
[SPEAKER_00]: But I never caught one in a major league.

1454
01:33:21,578 --> 01:33:24,700
[SPEAKER_00]: I caught several one-hitters and two-hitters, but not another no-hitter.

1455
01:33:27,252 --> 01:33:30,254
[SPEAKER_00]: I called no hitter, I believe I would have remembered it.

1456
01:33:30,494 --> 01:33:34,897
[SPEAKER_01]: Who were some of the easiest pitchers to catch at Detroit?

1457
01:33:36,158 --> 01:33:37,980
[SPEAKER_00]: Like Bridges?

1458
01:33:38,140 --> 01:33:40,561
[SPEAKER_00]: Crowder was very easy to catch.

1459
01:33:42,263 --> 01:33:45,485
[SPEAKER_00]: None of them are really difficult now at all.

1460
01:33:45,585 --> 01:33:48,507
[SPEAKER_00]: I feel like they were.

1461
01:33:51,089 --> 01:33:54,631
[SPEAKER_01]: Were you, did you catch any of those games at Rose, Winning Street there in 34?

1462
01:33:55,352 --> 01:33:55,672
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

1463
01:33:56,493 --> 01:33:57,033
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, of course.

1464
01:33:57,153 --> 01:33:58,134
[SPEAKER_00]: It was remarkable.

1465
01:33:58,274 --> 01:34:01,215
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, it was.

1466
01:34:01,255 --> 01:34:05,316
[SPEAKER_00]: I know one year we finished the season in Detroit, and Johnny Allen had won.

1467
01:34:06,696 --> 01:34:08,137
[SPEAKER_00]: He had tied the all-time record.

1468
01:34:09,697 --> 01:34:13,899
[SPEAKER_00]: But out that day, and we just figured out Allen had beat us, I caught the game.

1469
01:34:15,059 --> 01:34:23,142
[SPEAKER_00]: And they pitched, Bucky Harris pitched Jake Wade.

1470
01:34:23,742 --> 01:34:25,483
[SPEAKER_00]: Wasn't even a regular pitcher on our staff.

1471
01:34:25,643 --> 01:34:26,263
[SPEAKER_00]: Wow!

1472
01:34:26,998 --> 01:34:27,679
[SPEAKER_00]: Always wild.

1473
01:34:28,620 --> 01:34:31,022
[SPEAKER_01]: He went out that day and was darned if he didn't beat John Allen.

1474
01:34:40,750 --> 01:34:45,754
[SPEAKER_00]: Well that day Jake pitched a masterpiece and he beat Allen one to nothing.

1475
01:34:45,774 --> 01:34:46,875
[SPEAKER_01]: Broke his streak.

1476
01:34:46,915 --> 01:34:48,737
[SPEAKER_00]: He kept him from breaking the record.

1477
01:34:49,157 --> 01:34:54,081
[SPEAKER_00]: There's another little incident I'd like to tell you about.

1478
01:34:55,063 --> 01:34:59,904
[SPEAKER_00]: visiting with Charlie Garington, his wife, down in West Palm Beach one spring.

1479
01:34:59,944 --> 01:35:08,526
[SPEAKER_00]: My wife and I ran into him one afternoon and we got talking and Charlie and Marv Owen were all his great friends.

1480
01:35:08,606 --> 01:35:24,710
[SPEAKER_00]: So Charlie said that they had their, had a wedding anniversary and they got this very heavy package from Marv Owen and said when they opened it

1481
01:35:25,953 --> 01:35:34,880
[SPEAKER_00]: It was a brick out of the church that Charlie and his wife, of course, were married in out in California.

1482
01:35:35,520 --> 01:35:37,622
[SPEAKER_00]: And Marv Owen was their best man.

1483
01:35:38,242 --> 01:35:43,866
[SPEAKER_00]: And he went by this church one day and they were tearing it down, so he just went over and got a brick out of it and sent it to Charlie.

1484
01:35:44,287 --> 01:35:45,367
[SPEAKER_00]: And they got the brick down.

1485
01:35:45,387 --> 01:35:49,070
[SPEAKER_00]: They think it was the greatest thing ever happened for him to send a brick out of that church.

1486
01:35:53,497 --> 01:35:57,319
[SPEAKER_00]: One year I was a scout for Detroit and they called him into Detroit in the fall.

1487
01:35:58,199 --> 01:36:04,002
[SPEAKER_00]: And he came on from California with not many clothes on.

1488
01:36:04,022 --> 01:36:07,203
[SPEAKER_00]: They were in his suitcase and he got out there and he was about to freeze.

1489
01:36:08,023 --> 01:36:10,124
[SPEAKER_00]: Charlie Carrington loaned him his overcoat.

1490
01:36:10,985 --> 01:36:13,126
[SPEAKER_00]: And it was the nicest, warmest overcoat.

1491
01:36:13,166 --> 01:36:15,787
[SPEAKER_00]: He said he just wore it around there and wore it back home.

1492
01:36:16,752 --> 01:36:20,413
[SPEAKER_00]: I called Charlie one day and he said, Charlie, I want to send your coat back to you.

1493
01:36:20,433 --> 01:36:22,154
[SPEAKER_00]: Charlie said, forget it, keep it.

1494
01:36:23,194 --> 01:36:24,414
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a present for you.

1495
01:36:26,815 --> 01:36:39,279
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I was thinking about these stories they tell about Hogson and Gerringer who didn't talk much and they roomed together and they'd get up in the morning and say good morning and say good night at night.

1496
01:36:39,319 --> 01:36:41,080
[SPEAKER_01]: How true are those kind of stories?

1497
01:36:41,600 --> 01:36:43,060
[SPEAKER_00]: They're pretty much true.

1498
01:36:43,080 --> 01:36:44,241
[SPEAKER_00]: Neither one of them talked much.

1499
01:36:49,533 --> 01:36:51,034
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1500
01:36:51,654 --> 01:36:59,278
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, Charlie, Charlie had never talked much around in groups, but you could sit down and talk to him casually.

1501
01:36:59,698 --> 01:37:03,200
[SPEAKER_00]: Very easy to visit with and a very likable fellow to talk to.

1502
01:37:03,300 --> 01:37:06,402
[SPEAKER_00]: I enjoyed talking to him.

1503
01:37:07,722 --> 01:37:11,024
[SPEAKER_00]: But it all cut out a little effort, you know.

1504
01:37:11,084 --> 01:37:16,927
[SPEAKER_00]: You meet these fellas along the way and some of them things stand out in your mind that you remember.

1505
01:37:18,872 --> 01:37:20,774
[SPEAKER_01]: How about your biggest thrill in baseball?

1506
01:37:21,114 --> 01:37:22,435
[SPEAKER_00]: Has anything stand out to you?

1507
01:37:22,976 --> 01:37:24,997
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe down in the Texas league, that hot streak you had.

1508
01:37:25,017 --> 01:37:25,878
[SPEAKER_00]: That was a hot streak.

1509
01:37:25,938 --> 01:37:32,264
[SPEAKER_00]: I think probably one of the greatest thrills I ever had in baseball was breaking that record.

1510
01:37:33,905 --> 01:37:41,672
[SPEAKER_00]: And for a real thrill was the day that Gosling got his base hit and we won the 35 World Series.

1511
01:37:42,983 --> 01:37:44,304
[SPEAKER_00]: We wanted to win so much.

1512
01:37:44,344 --> 01:37:46,265
[SPEAKER_00]: We lost the year before to the Cardinals.

1513
01:37:46,285 --> 01:37:49,567
[SPEAKER_00]: The Deans beat us two games apiece in the 34 series.

1514
01:37:50,328 --> 01:37:52,409
[SPEAKER_00]: Knocked us out of getting the World Series ring.

1515
01:37:52,429 --> 01:37:55,371
[SPEAKER_00]: So the next year around, we won that World Series ring.

1516
01:37:55,431 --> 01:37:59,234
[SPEAKER_00]: And it was just a great thrill to win it, knowing that we were going to get it.

1517
01:38:00,014 --> 01:38:01,956
[SPEAKER_00]: So we did.

1518
01:38:02,036 --> 01:38:05,218
[SPEAKER_00]: And it was a crowning thing for us.

1519
01:38:06,198 --> 01:38:10,001
[SPEAKER_00]: So that, among many other nice gifts that we got.

1520
01:38:11,558 --> 01:38:13,319
[SPEAKER_00]: You bought some property out here too, I understand.

1521
01:38:14,139 --> 01:38:16,599
[SPEAKER_00]: Here's the thing on my desk you might like to see.

1522
01:38:16,639 --> 01:38:20,420
[SPEAKER_00]: This very beautiful thing was given to us in Detroit.

1523
01:38:22,221 --> 01:38:23,081
[SPEAKER_00]: Kind of a plaque.

1524
01:38:24,101 --> 01:38:30,083
[SPEAKER_00]: And I have in that drawer there my lifetime pass in baseball.

1525
01:38:32,303 --> 01:38:33,284
[SPEAKER_00]: You might want to see.

1526
01:38:33,344 --> 01:38:34,704
[SPEAKER_00]: I know you've seen them before.

1527
01:38:35,304 --> 01:38:38,025
[SPEAKER_00]: Let me just hand this out to you.

1528
01:38:38,705 --> 01:38:39,145
[SPEAKER_00]: Here it is.

1529
01:38:41,505 --> 01:38:43,427
[SPEAKER_00]: I was awfully proud to get that.

1530
01:38:53,775 --> 01:39:00,199
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that 34 series, though, was that when they started throwing the stuff at Medrick?

1531
01:39:00,240 --> 01:39:10,127
[SPEAKER_00]: Medrick slid into Marvel and Third Base and upended him, and the fans were so disturbed about it, they wouldn't let Medrick come back to left field.

1532
01:39:10,956 --> 01:39:16,438
[SPEAKER_00]: And they threw, I don't know where they got all the vegetables and oranges and apples and things from.

1533
01:39:16,458 --> 01:39:17,579
[SPEAKER_00]: They came ready, anyhow.

1534
01:39:17,599 --> 01:39:19,559
[SPEAKER_00]: They covered the field with it.

1535
01:39:20,420 --> 01:39:27,923
[SPEAKER_00]: And Judge Landis, of course, called Medrick over and asked him some questions and, of course, banished him from the game.

1536
01:39:28,963 --> 01:39:32,585
[SPEAKER_00]: And they had to put in somebody from Medrick to finish the game.

1537
01:39:32,605 --> 01:39:38,687
[SPEAKER_00]: But it was a double dose of bad feeling because

1538
01:39:39,143 --> 01:39:43,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Everybody knew the Cardinals already had us beat that day when this thing happened.

1539
01:39:44,624 --> 01:39:45,644
[SPEAKER_00]: Lopsided score.

1540
01:39:45,684 --> 01:39:46,384
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, it was.

1541
01:39:46,664 --> 01:39:48,145
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, they went down to the final game.

1542
01:39:48,165 --> 01:39:49,005
[SPEAKER_01]: Were you in that game?

1543
01:39:50,445 --> 01:39:50,725
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

1544
01:39:51,205 --> 01:39:53,226
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, at the end.

1545
01:39:53,586 --> 01:39:55,566
[SPEAKER_00]: Fresh beat us at ballgame that day.

1546
01:39:55,586 --> 01:39:55,946
[SPEAKER_00]: Went up.

1547
01:39:57,386 --> 01:40:02,587
[SPEAKER_00]: I think he hit a double or triple down the right field line off of Auker.

1548
01:40:02,647 --> 01:40:04,488
[SPEAKER_00]: Really broke the game open.

1549
01:40:06,852 --> 01:40:09,773
[SPEAKER_01]: Helman Auker, there's a name we haven't mentioned.

1550
01:40:09,833 --> 01:40:13,754
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, Auker, I caught the pennant winning game in 1935.

1551
01:40:14,034 --> 01:40:15,935
[SPEAKER_00]: We were playing the Browns in Detroit.

1552
01:40:16,675 --> 01:40:18,856
[SPEAKER_00]: Auker beat them 2 to 1 to clinch the pennant.

1553
01:40:21,097 --> 01:40:25,818
[SPEAKER_00]: Auker was a, he and Hogshead were identical on the one with the right hand and one with the left hand.

1554
01:40:25,838 --> 01:40:30,220
[SPEAKER_00]: They were underhand pitchers and threw sinkers all the time.

1555
01:40:31,220 --> 01:40:34,781
[SPEAKER_00]: Threw climbing curveballs and they were really tough pitchers to hit.

1556
01:40:36,127 --> 01:40:37,828
[SPEAKER_00]: kind of tough to catch, wasn't it?

1557
01:40:37,848 --> 01:40:39,268
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it was tough to catch.

1558
01:40:40,929 --> 01:40:45,591
[SPEAKER_00]: But we were fortunate to have him on our side, both of them.

1559
01:40:46,831 --> 01:40:48,212
[SPEAKER_00]: They were good men, good pitchers.

1560
01:40:48,412 --> 01:40:50,092
[SPEAKER_00]: That offer really went way down.

1561
01:40:50,252 --> 01:40:50,633
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

1562
01:40:51,113 --> 01:40:53,173
[SPEAKER_00]: And that was due to a football injury.

1563
01:40:53,193 --> 01:40:56,035
[SPEAKER_00]: He was a great football player.

1564
01:40:56,055 --> 01:41:00,696
[SPEAKER_00]: He was a great college player in football out in the University of Kansas.

1565
01:41:01,997 --> 01:41:04,898
[SPEAKER_00]: And he had a shoulder injury, and then

1566
01:41:05,360 --> 01:41:07,222
[SPEAKER_00]: And he was a pitcher, of course, at that time.

1567
01:41:07,242 --> 01:41:08,883
[SPEAKER_00]: So he had to go down and throw underhanded.

1568
01:41:10,145 --> 01:41:14,068
[SPEAKER_00]: And from that time on, he became an outstanding underhanded pitcher.

1569
01:41:14,088 --> 01:41:15,390
[SPEAKER_01]: He couldn't raise his arm up, right?

1570
01:41:15,530 --> 01:41:15,810
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

1571
01:41:16,090 --> 01:41:18,613
[SPEAKER_01]: It didn't affect his velocity or control.

1572
01:41:18,693 --> 01:41:19,494
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd come under there.

1573
01:41:19,534 --> 01:41:21,215
[SPEAKER_00]: And of course, Hogs would come under there, too.

1574
01:41:21,235 --> 01:41:23,137
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't know that about Hogs.

1575
01:41:24,138 --> 01:41:30,204
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, they always would love to have

1576
01:41:31,173 --> 01:41:36,416
[SPEAKER_00]: came across a young prospect somewhere in my scouting experience that flew like that, underhand.

1577
01:41:37,136 --> 01:41:39,677
[SPEAKER_00]: I never did see him.

1578
01:41:39,697 --> 01:41:40,758
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody was down under.

1579
01:41:41,699 --> 01:41:43,680
[SPEAKER_00]: All on top, three-quarters now.

1580
01:41:45,240 --> 01:41:49,343
[SPEAKER_01]: Was there any feeling about Hogshead or any discrimination because he was an Indian?

1581
01:41:49,363 --> 01:41:50,303
[SPEAKER_01]: None at all.

1582
01:41:50,343 --> 01:41:51,784
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

1583
01:41:52,864 --> 01:41:55,806
[SPEAKER_00]: No, he was a very likable fellow in our club.

1584
01:41:59,208 --> 01:41:59,528
[SPEAKER_00]: He was

1585
01:42:00,653 --> 01:42:01,734
[SPEAKER_00]: He had a good sense of humor.

1586
01:42:01,754 --> 01:42:08,238
[SPEAKER_00]: He wasn't one of those staid sort of individuals that was an Indian chief or anything, just a regular fella.

1587
01:42:09,599 --> 01:42:10,560
[SPEAKER_00]: He was a good guy.

1588
01:42:11,020 --> 01:42:12,721
[SPEAKER_00]: Was Art Herring with the team?

1589
01:42:12,761 --> 01:42:13,041
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

1590
01:42:13,422 --> 01:42:15,103
[SPEAKER_00]: Art pitched there for a few years.

1591
01:42:15,723 --> 01:42:21,127
[SPEAKER_01]: He lives out in, I think it's someplace in Indiana.

1592
01:42:22,488 --> 01:42:27,691
[SPEAKER_01]: I wanted to stop and see him, but I haven't been able to yet.

1593
01:42:28,111 --> 01:42:29,052
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I'd like to see him.

1594
01:42:29,723 --> 01:42:34,244
[SPEAKER_00]: across paths with some of these fellas, but... Well, you must have run into a few of them while you were scouting.

1595
01:42:34,785 --> 01:42:35,465
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, I did.

1596
01:42:35,765 --> 01:42:36,105
[SPEAKER_00]: I did.

1597
01:42:36,205 --> 01:42:40,487
[SPEAKER_00]: And fellas are former players in the scouting field.

1598
01:42:42,067 --> 01:42:43,948
[SPEAKER_00]: I've been seeing a lot of those fellas around.

1599
01:42:43,968 --> 01:42:47,089
[SPEAKER_00]: They come around here sometimes.

1600
01:42:47,209 --> 01:42:50,130
[SPEAKER_00]: We have several Major League Scouts in this area here.

1601
01:42:50,670 --> 01:42:52,590
[SPEAKER_00]: Gil English, of course, is retired now.

1602
01:42:52,730 --> 01:42:54,251
[SPEAKER_00]: He's scouted for many, many years.

1603
01:42:54,831 --> 01:42:57,292
[SPEAKER_00]: The Milwaukee and the Giants, the Yankees.

1604
01:42:58,500 --> 01:43:02,481
[SPEAKER_00]: And Mace Brown, he still is active with the Red Sox.

1605
01:43:03,561 --> 01:43:10,783
[SPEAKER_00]: And Billy Smith has scouted for us for our organization a long time in Milwaukee.

1606
01:43:10,803 --> 01:43:13,884
[SPEAKER_00]: He's now with Houston, been down there for about 10 years.

1607
01:43:13,924 --> 01:43:16,505
[SPEAKER_00]: He lives right here, about four or five miles from here.

1608
01:43:16,905 --> 01:43:24,967
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, we have about half a dozen fellas right around here that some of them are retired, some of them are still active, but they're all my golfing buddies.

1609
01:43:25,670 --> 01:43:26,990
[SPEAKER_01]: We all go out there and play golf.

1610
01:43:27,010 --> 01:43:30,052
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you scout college games?

1611
01:43:30,552 --> 01:43:31,932
[SPEAKER_00]: They do, my brother and all.

1612
01:43:31,952 --> 01:43:33,933
[SPEAKER_00]: They scout high school, college, legion.

1613
01:43:35,333 --> 01:43:37,454
[SPEAKER_01]: We have a pretty good college team up where I am.

1614
01:43:37,494 --> 01:43:42,236
[SPEAKER_01]: We've gone quite far in the Division III of the NCAA for a couple years.

1615
01:43:42,256 --> 01:43:47,918
[SPEAKER_01]: In fact, we have the World Series, I think, called the National Championship for Division III, the smallest school in Marietta.

1616
01:43:48,638 --> 01:43:50,379
[SPEAKER_01]: And that draws quite a few scouts up there.

1617
01:43:55,050 --> 01:43:59,832
[SPEAKER_01]: I mentioned, I asked you just when we snapped the other tape, Bob, about batting against Walter Johnson.

1618
01:44:00,332 --> 01:44:00,793
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh yes.

1619
01:44:01,633 --> 01:44:04,634
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, I remember hitting against Walter Johnson very distinctly.

1620
01:44:05,134 --> 01:44:11,817
[SPEAKER_00]: I should have because I'd heard so much about him as a kid growing up and how hard he could throw and all.

1621
01:44:12,778 --> 01:44:15,399
[SPEAKER_00]: I got a great kick out of hitting against him.

1622
01:44:16,479 --> 01:44:18,640
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll tell you a little story about him you might want to hear.

1623
01:44:19,580 --> 01:44:23,222
[SPEAKER_00]: One day Whitlow Watt and I were sitting on the bench in Detroit

1624
01:44:23,764 --> 01:44:25,124
[SPEAKER_00]: This is back in the early 30s.

1625
01:44:26,044 --> 01:44:32,786
[SPEAKER_00]: And Walter Johnson came out through our dugout, as players did then, and he stopped to talk a minute.

1626
01:44:33,586 --> 01:44:34,966
[SPEAKER_01]: He was the manager for Washington?

1627
01:44:35,026 --> 01:44:35,346
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

1628
01:44:35,846 --> 01:44:47,388
[SPEAKER_00]: And we said, Walter, back in your heyday, when you first came up to the Major Leagues, we're just wondering, how hard could you throw?

1629
01:44:47,408 --> 01:44:51,649
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, he said, boys, I could throw just as hard as I wanted.

1630
01:44:52,838 --> 01:45:01,461
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, if I wanted to really reach back and get it, I could just throw as hard as, meaning that if he had to get a follow out, he'd just strike him out.

1631
01:45:02,201 --> 01:45:07,383
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, back in those days when Washington was finishing last every year, he'd win 20, 25 games.

1632
01:45:08,604 --> 01:45:18,247
[SPEAKER_00]: And I believe what he said, because at that time, we'd sit on the bench and watch him pitch batting practice against his own club.

1633
01:45:18,809 --> 01:45:21,811
[SPEAKER_00]: And we all agreed that he could throw harder than anybody else.

1634
01:45:21,831 --> 01:45:24,592
[SPEAKER_01]: His pitching staff said that he was already through as a pitcher.

1635
01:45:25,593 --> 01:45:28,234
[SPEAKER_01]: But he was a big sidewinder down here.

1636
01:45:29,095 --> 01:45:30,716
[SPEAKER_01]: He must have never had an arm problem.

1637
01:45:30,776 --> 01:45:32,397
[SPEAKER_00]: He never got tired.

1638
01:45:32,797 --> 01:45:38,920
[SPEAKER_00]: He could throw hard even long towards the end of his career when I had a chance to hit against him a couple of times.

1639
01:45:38,940 --> 01:45:41,282
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you get a hit?

1640
01:45:41,302 --> 01:45:41,562
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

1641
01:45:41,582 --> 01:45:43,323
[SPEAKER_00]: I hit two balls pretty good off of him.

1642
01:45:44,132 --> 01:45:49,877
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't remember getting a hit off of him, but if you ask me about Bob Feller, I can remember the hits I got off of him, but not too many.

1643
01:45:51,239 --> 01:45:54,141
[SPEAKER_00]: I think Bob Feller was the toughest pitcher I ever faced in my life.

1644
01:45:54,782 --> 01:45:56,964
[SPEAKER_00]: He was so difficult to follow.

1645
01:45:58,105 --> 01:45:58,886
[SPEAKER_00]: All his motions.

1646
01:45:58,906 --> 01:46:02,850
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, his whole delivery was back of him, and it wasn't bothering me so much.

1647
01:46:02,870 --> 01:46:08,515
[SPEAKER_00]: He had a great curveball, he could throw hard, but he pitched all the time looking out in the right field.

1648
01:46:09,192 --> 01:46:13,113
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, if you just look around at me, that worried me.

1649
01:46:13,133 --> 01:46:15,634
[SPEAKER_00]: I was afraid you were going to lose sight of me at the plate.

1650
01:46:17,095 --> 01:46:19,636
[SPEAKER_01]: But Ty Cobb batted for you though once.

1651
01:46:19,656 --> 01:46:25,457
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, we were playing a game in Detroit, and I had faced Johnson once or twice.

1652
01:46:25,477 --> 01:46:31,780
[SPEAKER_00]: We got in a situation where there were a couple men on, so Cobb pinch hit for me and hit a double.

1653
01:46:32,460 --> 01:46:33,900
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember that very distinctly.

1654
01:46:35,021 --> 01:46:39,042
[SPEAKER_00]: And I didn't feel bad about that at all, as I said earlier.

1655
01:46:39,965 --> 01:46:44,027
[SPEAKER_00]: But it was a great thrill to hit against a pitcher so great as Johnson.

1656
01:46:46,268 --> 01:46:50,529
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, this has been a lot of fun, and I sure enjoyed it.

1657
01:46:50,589 --> 01:46:52,070
[SPEAKER_01]: I just wish I could stay more.

1658
01:46:52,630 --> 01:46:59,293
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's a real pleasure just to sit down and reminisce here and hit some of the highlights over all these years.

1659
01:46:59,313 --> 01:47:00,513
[SPEAKER_01]: You've had such a career, though.

1660
01:47:00,533 --> 01:47:02,194
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm sure you've left out an awful lot.

1661
01:47:02,314 --> 01:47:03,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, heavens, I can't.

1662
01:47:03,575 --> 01:47:06,476
[SPEAKER_01]: The tape is so short.

1663
01:47:06,496 --> 01:47:09,177
[SPEAKER_00]: It'd take a lot of sessions to catch up on that.

1664
01:47:09,581 --> 01:47:15,544
[SPEAKER_00]: I wish sometime that you could see the scrapbooks and the pictures and things that are here.

1665
01:47:16,024 --> 01:47:23,327
[SPEAKER_00]: My grandsons now keep asking me, he says, Papa, why don't you make a Hall of Fame, put all your pictures right up on the wall.

1666
01:47:24,047 --> 01:47:25,728
[SPEAKER_00]: I said, well, maybe we'll do it someday.

1667
01:47:25,748 --> 01:47:28,049
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that'd be great.

1668
01:47:28,089 --> 01:47:28,489
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1669
01:47:36,312 --> 01:47:37,253
[SPEAKER_02]: It is now about

1670
01:47:38,500 --> 01:47:42,682
[SPEAKER_02]: almost three weeks later from the time this tape was recorded.

1671
01:47:44,523 --> 01:47:56,147
[SPEAKER_02]: That was at High Point, North Carolina in Ray Hayworth's home on Monday, March 13, 1978.

1672
01:47:56,708 --> 01:48:03,170
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm now back here at home where I've been for quite a while, and it's Friday evening, March 31, 1978.

1673
01:48:03,190 --> 01:48:03,991
[SPEAKER_02]: I didn't have a chance to

1674
01:48:07,749 --> 01:48:13,293
[SPEAKER_02]: comment on the tape at the time, and I forgot about it when we got back here and had other things to do.

1675
01:48:14,815 --> 01:48:15,976
[SPEAKER_02]: So I don't have anything to do now.

1676
01:48:17,117 --> 01:48:26,884
[SPEAKER_02]: I do have a few things, but I thought I'd get a little bit down on this tape here about Ray Hayworth.

1677
01:48:29,727 --> 01:48:36,232
[SPEAKER_02]: As I recall it, I left Ernie Shore's home around one o'clock or so.

1678
01:48:37,681 --> 01:48:42,243
[SPEAKER_02]: And trying to get out of Winston-Salem, I got lost.

1679
01:48:45,244 --> 01:48:46,224
[SPEAKER_02]: I didn't really get lost.

1680
01:48:46,264 --> 01:48:48,805
[SPEAKER_02]: I did make one or two wrong turns.

1681
01:48:49,826 --> 01:48:51,826
[SPEAKER_02]: But I finally made it out of town there.

1682
01:48:53,187 --> 01:48:58,329
[SPEAKER_02]: And the high point is only about 15 or 20 miles away on the road south.

1683
01:49:00,008 --> 01:49:22,390
[SPEAKER_02]: little southeast of Winston-Salem, so I drove down there, and I knew that the road from my conversation the day before with Ray Hayworth, when I set up the interview, I knew that the road off of the main road out to his home was north of town several miles, so I kept my eye open for roads leading off to the left as I drove down, but I

1684
01:49:23,298 --> 01:49:27,081
[SPEAKER_02]: Finally got into High Point in the outskirts, and I drove into a service station.

1685
01:49:27,201 --> 01:49:28,762
[SPEAKER_02]: Asked where this one road was.

1686
01:49:28,802 --> 01:49:29,983
[SPEAKER_02]: I've forgotten now the name of it.

1687
01:49:31,904 --> 01:49:36,727
[SPEAKER_02]: And they said it was back about half a mile, so I hadn't really gone too far in.

1688
01:49:36,747 --> 01:49:39,889
[SPEAKER_02]: So I worked my way back and then out this road several miles.

1689
01:49:39,929 --> 01:49:44,813
[SPEAKER_02]: And I came to Westminster Village, which I think that's some pretty good recaller, isn't it?

1690
01:49:44,873 --> 01:49:49,756
[SPEAKER_02]: I think Westminster Village is the name of the little development where he is located.

1691
01:49:49,856 --> 01:49:52,598
[SPEAKER_02]: And I found the road.

1692
01:49:53,046 --> 01:49:55,127
[SPEAKER_02]: that he mentioned and weave my way around.

1693
01:49:55,928 --> 01:50:03,112
[SPEAKER_02]: It was a very sparsely populated area, a few farmhouses scattered about, very nicely maintained.

1694
01:50:04,072 --> 01:50:10,636
[SPEAKER_02]: As I pulled into the driveway here, there was an old gentleman out working in a wood pile and getting some things organized.

1695
01:50:10,716 --> 01:50:12,457
[SPEAKER_02]: I didn't know if that was Ray Hayworth or not.

1696
01:50:14,038 --> 01:50:19,942
[SPEAKER_02]: But I pulled into the parking space there and he came over and piled his wood up and then came to me

1697
01:50:21,348 --> 01:50:25,129
[SPEAKER_02]: Greeted me as Mr. Murdock, and I greeted him as Ray Hayworth.

1698
01:50:25,169 --> 01:50:28,190
[SPEAKER_02]: So he was the right party.

1699
01:50:28,710 --> 01:50:30,911
[SPEAKER_02]: Very congenial and friendly.

1700
01:50:32,171 --> 01:50:36,553
[SPEAKER_02]: A pretty big man, although as a player he didn't strike me as very big.

1701
01:50:36,573 --> 01:50:39,133
[SPEAKER_02]: He was rather lanky and lean.

1702
01:50:40,034 --> 01:50:42,254
[SPEAKER_02]: And he's somewhat lanky and not so lean now.

1703
01:50:42,714 --> 01:50:46,275
[SPEAKER_02]: Probably about six feet and filled out quite a bit.

1704
01:50:46,575 --> 01:50:48,096
[SPEAKER_02]: He's 73 years old, remember.

1705
01:50:49,088 --> 01:50:55,530
[SPEAKER_02]: But ruddy complexion, full face, a couple of chins, which he never had as a ball player.

1706
01:50:56,670 --> 01:50:59,770
[SPEAKER_02]: But good health and robust.

1707
01:50:59,811 --> 01:51:01,951
[SPEAKER_02]: And he works outdoors a great deal.

1708
01:51:03,011 --> 01:51:08,413
[SPEAKER_02]: We chatted briefly out in the driveway.

1709
01:51:09,453 --> 01:51:18,775
[SPEAKER_02]: And he, at that time, mentioned about some of this land he had inherited and quite a bit more he had purchased with his 1934 World Series check.

1710
01:51:19,983 --> 01:51:31,653
[SPEAKER_02]: that his son is a public official in High Point, something to do with subdivisions, when property is subdivided and sold.

1711
01:51:32,473 --> 01:51:38,598
[SPEAKER_02]: And so with his son's assistance, he worked out some plan by which much of this land was divided and sold.

1712
01:51:38,658 --> 01:51:41,020
[SPEAKER_02]: So they've made money, all right.

1713
01:51:41,040 --> 01:51:42,321
[SPEAKER_02]: The house is a beautiful house.

1714
01:51:43,382 --> 01:51:45,224
[SPEAKER_02]: And they have nice things.

1715
01:51:45,264 --> 01:51:48,687
[SPEAKER_02]: So he's a man of considerable wealth based on land.

1716
01:51:49,553 --> 01:51:51,315
[SPEAKER_02]: and the selling of some of this land.

1717
01:51:52,375 --> 01:51:55,578
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, we went in the house.

1718
01:51:55,638 --> 01:51:56,899
[SPEAKER_02]: He had to change his boots.

1719
01:51:56,959 --> 01:52:01,303
[SPEAKER_02]: He didn't want to track his muddy boots all over the house, so he changed them.

1720
01:52:01,323 --> 01:52:02,504
[SPEAKER_02]: And then we went into the living room.

1721
01:52:02,544 --> 01:52:11,110
[SPEAKER_02]: I met his wife, and I met a, I think his wife's sister, who must live in the area, and some other lady.

1722
01:52:11,391 --> 01:52:13,833
[SPEAKER_02]: There were three women in the kitchen, and they were very cordial.

1723
01:52:14,593 --> 01:52:15,053
[SPEAKER_02]: pleasant.

1724
01:52:15,513 --> 01:52:27,495
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, then I think we should go upstairs and talk in his den because he has some of his baseball things up there and we wouldn't be disturbing anybody else up in his room.

1725
01:52:28,475 --> 01:52:29,295
[SPEAKER_02]: So we went up there.

1726
01:52:29,596 --> 01:52:34,156
[SPEAKER_02]: The house was very nice, as I say, beautiful carpeting, very fine furniture.

1727
01:52:34,216 --> 01:52:36,377
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't know what style or anything like that.

1728
01:52:36,397 --> 01:52:43,978
[SPEAKER_02]: I just knew it was very handsome looking and everything was spotless and rather plush.

1729
01:52:45,795 --> 01:52:50,799
[SPEAKER_02]: A lot of nice windows and views out into the countryside.

1730
01:52:51,560 --> 01:52:54,142
[SPEAKER_02]: Lovely chairs and tables.

1731
01:52:55,624 --> 01:52:56,985
[SPEAKER_02]: Pictures on the walls.

1732
01:52:57,025 --> 01:52:59,788
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't know what they were now.

1733
01:53:00,788 --> 01:53:01,789
[SPEAKER_02]: Lovely carpeting.

1734
01:53:02,530 --> 01:53:07,875
[SPEAKER_02]: It was just a very comfortable and rather rich-looking house.

1735
01:53:09,184 --> 01:53:16,445
[SPEAKER_02]: So we went upstairs and the same kind of impression I got about the rest of the house, very rich and comfortable and well upholstered.

1736
01:53:18,186 --> 01:53:28,148
[SPEAKER_02]: We went into his room and there's his bedroom there and we just sat down at a desk and started in and he was very easy to interview.

1737
01:53:29,328 --> 01:53:38,170
[SPEAKER_02]: He was knowledgeable, he was good memory, expresses himself very well.

1738
01:53:39,613 --> 01:53:41,654
[SPEAKER_02]: picked up the questions quite readily.

1739
01:53:43,094 --> 01:53:49,717
[SPEAKER_02]: He did get off a little too much, I thought, on his recent scouting history, which is not of great interest to me.

1740
01:53:49,797 --> 01:53:52,218
[SPEAKER_02]: I want to talk about his playing days, the old days.

1741
01:53:53,418 --> 01:54:01,782
[SPEAKER_02]: And his story about how he recruited Ernie Banks was an interesting one, but he could have been telling us so many more important things, I felt.

1742
01:54:08,293 --> 01:54:12,355
[SPEAKER_02]: The interview would have gone on much longer, I'm sure, if I hadn't been pressed for time.

1743
01:54:13,815 --> 01:54:21,418
[SPEAKER_02]: But I didn't even make an attempt to see his scrapbooks, which he mentioned towards the end of the tape there, or his pictures.

1744
01:54:21,458 --> 01:54:23,259
[SPEAKER_02]: There are a few pictures up in the room there.

1745
01:54:24,440 --> 01:54:26,620
[SPEAKER_02]: But he didn't have any he could give me an autograph.

1746
01:54:26,640 --> 01:54:31,482
[SPEAKER_02]: He says if he gets some or if he finds some that he can spare, he will autograph one and send it to me.

1747
01:54:31,522 --> 01:54:37,745
[SPEAKER_02]: But it was so contrary to my morning's experience over at Ernie Shores where I walked out with just about everything Ernie Shores had.

1748
01:54:38,574 --> 01:54:39,634
[SPEAKER_02]: And I still have it up here.

1749
01:54:39,654 --> 01:54:43,436
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm getting ready to return the shore stuff this next week.

1750
01:54:44,056 --> 01:54:53,039
[SPEAKER_02]: I've gotten newspapers repaired and copied, pictures copied, and so forth.

1751
01:54:53,079 --> 01:54:55,300
[SPEAKER_02]: So I'm ready to return the bundle.

1752
01:54:56,360 --> 01:54:57,700
[SPEAKER_02]: And we'll do it.

1753
01:54:58,141 --> 01:55:02,802
[SPEAKER_02]: I got most of the pictures copied by Phil Mazzara over in the College Information Office.

1754
01:55:02,842 --> 01:55:05,543
[SPEAKER_02]: About 32 7x9s made into 5x7s.

1755
01:55:09,132 --> 01:55:13,916
[SPEAKER_02]: Had I done this with my commercial photographer, it would have run up to $75 at least.

1756
01:55:13,936 --> 01:55:16,939
[SPEAKER_02]: I'd say Phil did it as a favor.

1757
01:55:19,001 --> 01:55:21,263
[SPEAKER_02]: But I'll be sending that back this week.

1758
01:55:21,343 --> 01:55:22,864
[SPEAKER_02]: I didn't get anything, though, from Ray Hayworth.

1759
01:55:22,884 --> 01:55:26,667
[SPEAKER_02]: I had a real good interview with Ray Hayworth, but no pictures of scrapbooks.

1760
01:55:26,727 --> 01:55:30,411
[SPEAKER_02]: With Ernie Shore, I had a rather poor interview, but all kinds of scrapbooks and pictures.

1761
01:55:31,952 --> 01:55:35,015
[SPEAKER_01]: So I guess it takes all kinds there.

1762
01:55:35,295 --> 01:55:36,516
[SPEAKER_02]: One thing supplements another.

1763
01:55:38,977 --> 01:55:45,402
[SPEAKER_02]: I was, we finished the 90 minutes and I practically, I think I closed the recorder off about that time.

1764
01:55:45,422 --> 01:55:50,145
[SPEAKER_02]: Then he started telling some stories so I slapped this present tape on to pick up a few more things.

1765
01:55:51,927 --> 01:55:55,109
[SPEAKER_02]: And the time was beginning to press though.

1766
01:55:55,149 --> 01:56:05,617
[SPEAKER_02]: The interview probably didn't get started around 2.30 after I got there around 2 and getting introduced and getting, meeting the ladies and getting upstairs, setting the recorder up.

1767
01:56:05,657 --> 01:56:06,838
[SPEAKER_02]: It probably wasn't about 2.30 that I

1768
01:56:07,998 --> 01:56:24,171
[SPEAKER_02]: got things we actually started and therefore the hour and a half interview on tape one here must have taken till around four o'clock and then I put this other one on for another 20 minutes or so and I was troubled though by the time because I had a three-hour drive ahead of me.

1769
01:56:25,052 --> 01:56:32,638
[SPEAKER_02]: I didn't want to get on that bad stretch of road between Siler City and Sanford where they have no white road markers.

1770
01:56:32,678 --> 01:56:33,758
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a newly serviced road.

1771
01:56:33,778 --> 01:56:35,500
[SPEAKER_02]: They don't have any white markers along the

1772
01:56:36,340 --> 01:56:38,801
[SPEAKER_02]: outer edge of the road or the middle stripe.

1773
01:56:38,941 --> 01:56:47,645
[SPEAKER_02]: And driving down the previous Saturday night when we first went down to Fatville, I went off the road once, and it was nerve-wracking driving.

1774
01:56:47,685 --> 01:56:48,506
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't know where you are.

1775
01:56:49,006 --> 01:56:52,868
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't judge distances very well in the dark when there's no marking on the road.

1776
01:56:52,888 --> 01:56:55,589
[SPEAKER_02]: And I wanted to get back beyond that point when it got dark.

1777
01:56:56,309 --> 01:56:57,510
[SPEAKER_02]: Hopefully, I wanted to get all the way back.

1778
01:56:59,111 --> 01:57:04,233
[SPEAKER_02]: And so leaving at 4.30, I had about two hours of daylight and about an hour of darkness in driving.

1779
01:57:05,862 --> 01:57:07,383
[SPEAKER_02]: So I didn't linger.

1780
01:57:07,804 --> 01:57:11,006
[SPEAKER_02]: I did cut this thing off when there was still a lot more to be done.

1781
01:57:12,647 --> 01:57:21,474
[SPEAKER_02]: I kind of felt that he was glad that I was cutting it off, and although he'd like to see me again, I think he thought we'd had enough of that one session.

1782
01:57:21,514 --> 01:57:34,784
[SPEAKER_02]: Maybe not, I don't know, but the fact is I had to get going, and so I only got about an hour and 45 to an hour and 15 minutes, including the first tape, with its 90 minutes and then the 15, 20 on this one.

1783
01:57:37,141 --> 01:57:44,263
[SPEAKER_02]: I had a feeling, while it was a good interview, we just didn't really get into some of the meat of the matter.

1784
01:57:44,303 --> 01:57:58,086
[SPEAKER_02]: So much that he's done, so much he's experienced, so many people he's played with and against, that there'd be a great deal more that could be done, and with his memory as good as it is now.

1785
01:57:59,426 --> 01:58:05,488
[SPEAKER_02]: He was really a wonderful person and very kind and thoughtful and polite and courteous

1786
01:58:08,469 --> 01:58:11,451
[SPEAKER_02]: I got pictures of them after the interview was over downstairs.

1787
01:58:11,491 --> 01:58:18,095
[SPEAKER_02]: He and his wife stood next to the living room fireplace or mantel, and I snapped a couple of pictures of them there.

1788
01:58:20,797 --> 01:58:25,040
[SPEAKER_02]: And he said he would send me any pictures he could find, autograph them.

1789
01:58:26,201 --> 01:58:32,085
[SPEAKER_02]: I haven't heard from him yet, but I presume I will be hearing from him if he does find anything.

1790
01:58:34,223 --> 01:58:53,252
[SPEAKER_02]: And so I packed up my gear and probably left around 20 to 5 or quarter to 5 in the afternoon and drove through High Point and took a road across from High Point over to that 87, is it, or 421, the road south from Greensboro to Fayetteville.

1791
01:58:54,432 --> 01:58:55,673
[SPEAKER_02]: I cut a little bit of time off.

1792
01:58:55,713 --> 01:58:57,914
[SPEAKER_02]: I didn't go back up north to Route 40.

1793
01:58:57,954 --> 01:58:59,895
[SPEAKER_01]: I cut across and hit the road going down.

1794
01:59:01,421 --> 01:59:04,923
[SPEAKER_02]: And made pretty good time, and got to Sanford just about dark.

1795
01:59:04,963 --> 01:59:15,048
[SPEAKER_02]: But it was 6.30 now, and so I called up and told them I would be another hour or so, or another 45 minutes anyhow, coming in from Sanford.

1796
01:59:15,508 --> 01:59:18,350
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that was an evening we planned to go out.

1797
01:59:18,390 --> 01:59:19,970
[SPEAKER_02]: We were going to take Gordon and Randy out.

1798
01:59:22,392 --> 01:59:23,752
[SPEAKER_02]: We hadn't really, but we might.

1799
01:59:23,992 --> 01:59:26,934
[SPEAKER_02]: So I was calling to say we were going to be a little late for that.

1800
01:59:27,834 --> 01:59:30,055
[SPEAKER_02]: Gordon says other plans had been made.

1801
01:59:30,256 --> 01:59:31,076
[SPEAKER_02]: He answered the phone.

1802
01:59:38,480 --> 01:59:49,588
[SPEAKER_02]: So I finally checked in around a quarter to seven that night and spent the evening poring over the Ernie Shore stuff, which is fabulous.

1803
01:59:49,608 --> 01:59:53,031
[SPEAKER_02]: It seems to me that's about it now.

1804
01:59:53,651 --> 02:00:04,920
[SPEAKER_02]: The Ray Awerth interview, I don't know if there's much more to add.

1805
02:00:07,550 --> 02:00:09,632
[SPEAKER_02]: He showed me a couple of things up in his room there.

1806
02:00:10,954 --> 02:00:12,876
[SPEAKER_02]: Some mementos of his, which are very nice.

1807
02:00:13,016 --> 02:00:22,346
[SPEAKER_02]: I think one of the Fort Worth team in the Texas League in 1946 or seven, he was manager there, with the pictures and names of some of the players.

1808
02:00:22,366 --> 02:00:23,727
[SPEAKER_02]: It was a Dodger farm club.

1809
02:00:24,388 --> 02:00:26,710
[SPEAKER_02]: And some of the players went on to great things.

1810
02:00:26,730 --> 02:00:28,452
[SPEAKER_02]: He mentions Duke Snyder in particular.

1811
02:00:30,559 --> 02:00:32,401
[SPEAKER_02]: He had great respect for Branch Rickey.

1812
02:00:32,441 --> 02:00:33,642
[SPEAKER_02]: I think that came out in the tape.

1813
02:00:33,662 --> 02:00:38,005
[SPEAKER_02]: He worked with Rickey as a Fort Worth manager and in the Dodger organization, maybe as a scout.

1814
02:00:38,566 --> 02:00:39,267
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, as a scout.

1815
02:00:39,287 --> 02:00:42,489
[SPEAKER_02]: He scouted some of those early black players and some later ones, too.

1816
02:00:50,937 --> 02:00:52,378
[SPEAKER_02]: So I tried to get him to

1817
02:00:54,201 --> 02:00:57,663
[SPEAKER_02]: And he did answer the question about how he felt when Cochran came to Detroit.

1818
02:00:57,683 --> 02:00:59,304
[SPEAKER_02]: And he handled it very beautifully as well.

1819
02:00:59,384 --> 02:01:05,968
[SPEAKER_02]: I'd rather be a catcher and a pennant winner than a second string catcher and a pennant winner than a first stringer on a last place team, the St. Louis Browns.

1820
02:01:07,029 --> 02:01:08,549
[SPEAKER_02]: That was a pretty good reply.

1821
02:01:08,569 --> 02:01:12,612
[SPEAKER_02]: Maybe it's old, but it sounded good.

1822
02:01:13,933 --> 02:01:14,933
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's about it, I guess.

1823
02:01:14,993 --> 02:01:17,395
[SPEAKER_02]: I can't think of anything more here on the Hayworth interview.

1824
02:01:17,415 --> 02:01:21,217
[SPEAKER_02]: It's going to leave me a whole half a tape here with nothing on it.

1825
02:01:21,297 --> 02:01:23,238
[SPEAKER_02]: But maybe I'll think of something one of these days.

1826
02:01:23,864 --> 02:01:26,418
[SPEAKER_02]: Signing off now on the 31st of March 1978.

